:: StoryDisplayTitle {"position":"225,725","size":"100,100"}
$game.title
:: Ch1_Opening {"position":"325,625","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 1
The Festival of Lanterns, dawn
==================================================*/

<<set $game.time = "Dawn">>
<<set $game.location = "Amir's Home">>

The call to prayer finds you before the sun does.

It drifts over the rooftops of Zafirah in long, unhurried waves, and for a moment you lie still and let it pass through you the way you have every morning since you were a boy. Somewhere below, your mother is already awake -- you can smell cardamom and scorched sugar, which means she is burning the pastries again, which means she is nervous, which means today is the Festival of Lanterns and half the street will be at your door by midday wanting bread they didn't have to bake themselves.

Your father's pendant is cool against your collarbone. It always is, first thing, before your own warmth reaches it.

Dust hangs gold in the one shaft of light that gets past your shutter. A rooster somewhere is losing an argument with a dog. Zafirah is waking up loud and unbothered, the way it always does, as if it has never once considered that a day could go wrong.

You lie there a moment longer, listening to the city.

<<link "Rise before she has to call you">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	<<set $choices.wokeEarly = true>>
	<<goto "Ch1_Bedroom">>
<</link>>

<<link "Linger and listen to the prayer finish">>
	<<personality "faith" 1>>
	<<set $choices.wokeEarly = false>>
	<<goto "Ch1_Bedroom">>
<</link>>


:: Ch1_Bedroom {"position":"425,625","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 2
Getting ready
==================================================*/

<<if $choices.wokeEarly>>
	You're up before your mother thinks to shout for you, and there's a small, private satisfaction in that -- like winning an argument no one else knew you were having.
<<else>>
	The prayer finishes on a long note that seems to hang in the air after the voice has stopped. Only then do you swing your legs off the mat, unhurried, unwilling to let the morning start faster than it wants to.
<</if>>

The room is small and yours in every way that matters: a shelf of your father's old ledgers you've never had reason to open, a wooden practice sword worn pale at the grip, a cracked clay bowl you use for washing. You splash water on your face. It's already losing its overnight chill.

Your armor for the day is a traveler's coat gone soft at the elbows, and the training sword your uncle gave you the year you turned twelve, insisting -- wrongly, so far -- that a boy who could handle a blade would never be caught without work. You buckle it on anyway. Today there will be crowds, and crowds in Zafirah during festival season have a way of finding the one person not paying attention.

On the shelf, tucked behind the ledgers, sits a small brass compass you found half-buried near the river docks as a child. It's a plain, unremarkable thing -- dented casing, a hairline crack across the glass -- except that the needle has never once pointed north. You used to think it was simply broken. You still do, mostly.

<<link "Take the compass with you" "Ch1_Breakfast">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
	<<set $inventory.keyItems.push("Broken Compass")>>
	<<set $choices.tookCompass = true>>
	<<codex "items" "Broken Compass">>
<</link>>

<<link "Leave it where it is -- today isn't the day for distractions" "Ch1_Breakfast">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	<<set $choices.tookCompass = false>>
<</link>>


:: Ch1_Breakfast {"position":"525,625","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 3
Breakfast with Amir's mother
==================================================*/

<<set $game.location = "Amir's Home">>

Downstairs, the kitchen is a small war zone of flour and good intentions. Your mother, Farah, has flour to her elbows and a smear of it on one cheek she doesn't know about, and three trays of pastries in various states of not-quite-ruined.

"Sit," she says, without turning around, which means she heard you on the stairs before you'd finished the second step. "Eat something that isn't burnt. I have plenty that qualifies."

<<if $choices.wokeEarly>>
	She glances back at you, surprised despite herself. "You're up early." A small, pleased pause. "Don't get used to my noticing."
<<else>>
	"I let the prayer finish before I got up," you offer, before she can accuse you of laziness.
	"Mm," she says, unconvinced, sliding a plate at you anyway. "The Almighty can wait. My oven cannot."
<</if>>

You eat. The bread is good; the pastries, true to smell, are not. Farah doesn't sit -- she never does until the last tray is out -- but she talks while she works, the way she always has, filling the kitchen with news of who's fighting with whom over stall space in the market and whether the Vizier's household will actually send lanterns for the poor quarter this year like they promised.

"Yasmina came by yesterday," she adds, too casually, wiping her hands on her apron. "Asked after you. I told her you were busy being useless." A faint smile. "She didn't believe me."

<<if $choices.tookCompass>>
	Her eyes catch on the compass at your belt and narrow, just slightly. "You still have that thing." Not a question. "Your father hated that river. Never told me why."
	<<link "Ask her what she means" "Ch1_Street">>
		<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
		<<relationship "yasmina" 0>>
		<<set $knowledge.fatherRiverHint = true>>
		<<journal "Mother mentioned my father disliked the river. She wouldn't say more.">>
	<</link>>
	<<link "Let it go -- there's a festival to get to" "Ch1_Street">>
		<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	<</link>>
<<else>>
	<<link "Ask about Yasmina" "Ch1_Street">>
		<<personality "mercy" 1>>
		<<journal "Mother says Yasmina came looking for me.">>
	<</link>>
	<<link "Finish eating and head out -- the day won't wait" "Ch1_Street">>
		<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	<</link>>
<</if>>


:: Ch1_Street {"position":"625,625","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 2
Leaving Home
==================================================*/

<<set $game.location = "Zafirah -- Lantern Quarter">>

You step out into a street that has already forgotten it's early.

Paper lanterns hang from every doorframe and window lattice, still unlit, waiting for dusk to give them a reason. Someone has strung a line of them clear across the road, low enough that you have to duck under a dangling tassel of orange silk. The air smells of dust warmed by the coming sun, of frying dough from a stall two doors down, of the sharp green smell of palm fronds being woven into arches for tonight.

<<if $personality.faith gte 1>>
	The muezzin's call has faded, but you find yourself still walking a little slower than you need to, the way you always do on a morning like this.
<</if>>
<<if $choices.tookCompass>>
	The compass sits heavier in your pocket than it has any right to. Habit, you tell yourself, and keep walking.
<</if>>

Your neighborhood is small enough that you know most of the doors by the people behind them, and this morning every one of them is open.

<<goto "Ch1_Street_Hub">>


:: Ch1_Street_Hub {"position":"725,625","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 2 (cont.)
Neighborhood Hub
==================================================*/

<<set _visitedAll = ($choices.metBilal and $choices.metChildren and $choices.metGuard)>>

<<if !$choices.streetHubSeen>>
	<<set $choices.streetHubSeen = true>>
	You're in no real hurry yet. The marketplace will still be there in an hour, and there are worse ways to spend a festival morning than letting the street pull you along at its own pace.
<<else>>
	You linger a moment longer before moving on.
<</if>>

<<if !$choices.metBilal>>
* <<link "Old Bilal is arguing with a ladder outside his shop" "Ch1_Street_Bilal">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.metChildren>>
* <<link "A pair of children are racing lantern-frames down the gutter" "Ch1_Street_Children">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.metGuard>>
* <<link "A city guard is wrestling a garland onto the gate-post" "Ch1_Street_Guard">><</link>>
<</if>>

<<if _visitedAll>>
	You've said your good mornings. The street has nothing left to show you that it hasn't already.

	<<link "Head toward the marketplace" "Ch1_Street_End">><</link>>
<<else>>

	<<link "Leave the street behind and head toward the marketplace" "Ch1_Street_End">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: Ch1_Street_Bilal {"position":"825,525","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Minor NPC: Old Bilal, lantern-maker
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.metBilal = true>>
<<codex "people" "Bilal the Lantern-Maker">>

Old Bilal has been making lanterns in this quarter since before your father was born, and every year he insists this is the last one, and every year he is lying.

Right now he's three rungs up a ladder that has clearly opinions of its own, trying to hang a string of lanterns shaped like pomegranates over his shop door. The ladder disagrees with his balance. His balance is losing.

"Amir!" he calls down, not moving, as if calling your name will somehow steady the ladder. "You have two good hands and nothing better to do. Prove me right."

<<link "Steady the ladder for him" "Ch1_Street_Hub">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
	You cross and grip the ladder's legs. Bilal finishes the string of lanterns with the unhurried confidence of a man who was never actually in danger, then climbs down and cuffs your shoulder, hard, in what passes for gratitude.

	"Your father used to do the same," he says, "before he decided ladders were beneath him." He says it lightly. You've learned not to ask what he means when he says things like that.
<</link>>

<<link "Tell him to come down before he breaks something" "Ch1_Street_Hub">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	"I'm not climbing up there," you say. "Come down and I'll hold it from the ground."

	Bilal grumbles the whole way down about young men and their caution, but he comes down, and the ladder survives the morning, which is more than it deserved.
<</link>>


:: Ch1_Street_Children {"position":"825,625","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Minor NPC: neighborhood children
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.metChildren = true>>

Two children -- you're fairly sure one belongs to the fishmonger, the other to nobody in particular that anyone's ever confirmed -- are racing broken lantern-frames down the gutter like toy boats, shrieking with a level of investment usually reserved for actual warfare.

"Amir! Amir, judge us!" The fishmonger's boy skids to a stop in front of you, frame in hand. "Whoever's gets to the drain first wins."

<<link "Race them yourself, and win" "Ch1_Street_Hub">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	You crouch, take the smaller frame, and send it skimming down the gutter with the flick of a wrist you're secretly proud of. It beats both of theirs to the drain by a full second.

	The betrayal on their faces is immediate and total. "That's not fair, you're an *adult*," the girl informs you, as though this were cheating.
<</link>>

<<link "Let the smaller one win" "Ch1_Street_Hub">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
	You judge the race with great solemnity and declare the smaller of the two the winner, on the extremely scientific grounds that her frame "clearly had better aerodynamics." She does not question this. She simply accepts her victory as her due and runs off to inform everyone she knows.
<</link>>


:: Ch1_Street_Guard {"position":"825,725","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Minor NPC: city guard
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.metGuard = true>>

A guard in the Sultan's colors is losing a quiet, dignified battle with a garland of marigolds that refuses to sit straight on the gate-post. He's young -- younger than the post he's guarding, probably -- and trying very hard to look like a man who chose this posting rather than one who was assigned it.

He notices you watching and straightens, garland still crooked.

<<link "Greet him properly, as one would a soldier on duty" "Ch1_Street_Hub">>
	<<personality "honor" 1>>
	You incline your head the way your uncle taught you -- respect offered without servility. The guard blinks, visibly unused to it, then returns the gesture with more formality than the moment strictly requires. "Peace be upon you," he says, a little too crisply, and you leave him looking pleased with himself.
<</link>>

<<link "Point out the garland is crooked" "Ch1_Street_Hub">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
	"It's crooked," you say, by way of greeting.

	He looks up at it, then at you, with the flat expression of a man who has heard this six times already this morning. "I'm aware," he says. "Sultan's orders. Guard the gate. Nothing about the garland."

	You leave him to his war with the marigolds.
<</link>>


:: Ch1_Street_End {"position":"725,725","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 2 (end)
Toward the Marketplace
==================================================*/

<<set $game.location = "Zafirah -- Approaching the Festival Streets">>

The street opens ahead into a wider thoroughfare, and beyond it you can already hear the low, constant hum of Zafirah on festival morning -- hundreds of voices layered into one sound, the way the sea must sound to people who've never seen it.

Whatever today turns out to be, it's waiting for you down there.

<<link "Head into the festival streets" "Ch1_FestivalStreets">>
	<<journal "Left home on the morning of the Festival of Lanterns.">>
<</link>>


:: Ch1_FestivalStreets {"position":"625,825","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 3
Festival Streets
==================================================*/

<<set $game.location = "Zafirah -- Festival Streets">>

The thoroughfare swallows you into itself.

Zafirah on an ordinary morning is loud. Zafirah on a festival morning is something else entirely -- stalls doubling as they always do, but garlanded now in marigold and jasmine, awnings strung together overhead until the sky itself seems woven out of cloth. Somewhere a drum is being tuned, badly, one beat at a time. The whole city smells of rosewater and hot oil and, underneath it, the mineral hush of the river a few streets over.

You have all morning. Nobody is waiting on you yet.

<<goto "Ch1_FestivalStreets_Hub">>


:: Ch1_FestivalStreets_Hub {"position":"725,825","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 3 (cont.)
Festival Streets Hub
==================================================*/

<<set _visitedAll = ($choices.sawStoryteller and $choices.sawWeaver and $choices.sawMosque)>>

<<if !$choices.streetsHubSeen>>
	<<set $choices.streetsHubSeen = true>>
	You let yourself wander rather than walk with purpose.
<<else>>
	You take your time a while longer.
<</if>>

<<if !$choices.sawStoryteller>>
* <<link "A storyteller has drawn a small crowd near the fountain" "Ch1_FestivalStreets_Storyteller">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawWeaver>>
* <<link "Umm Layla's garland stall, bright with festival colors" "Ch1_FestivalStreets_Weaver">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawMosque>>
* <<link "The Great Mosque, cool and quiet past the noise" "Ch1_FestivalStreets_Mosque">><</link>>
<</if>>

<<if _visitedAll>>
	You've seen what the street has to offer this morning. The market is close now, and its noise is starting to outshout everything else.

	<<link "Head toward the Grand Marketplace" "Ch1_Yasmina">><</link>>
<<else>>

	<<link "Leave the streets behind and head toward the marketplace" "Ch1_Yasmina">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: Ch1_FestivalStreets_Storyteller {"position":"825,775","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Landmark: The Storyteller
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawStoryteller = true>>
<<codex "lore" "The Tale of the Scattered Treasure">>

An old man sits cross-legged on a threadbare rug, hands moving as much as his voice does, and a dozen children -- and at least as many adults pretending they're only stopping for a moment -- have gathered around him.

"...and so the wise ones looked upon the great treasure," he's saying, "and knew that no single hand, however honest, should ever hold all of it at once. So they broke it into seven pieces, and scattered them to the corners of the world, and charged each piece to a guardian who would test any who came seeking it -- not with steel, but with character."

A boy in front asks, too loudly, whether the treasure was gold. The old man laughs and says treasure is never gold, not really, and refuses to explain what he means.

<<link "Toss a coin into his bowl and move on" "Ch1_FestivalStreets_Hub">>
	<<personality "faith" 1>>
	<<loseGold 1>>
	You drop a small coin among the others already there. The old man doesn't break stride, but he nods at you between sentences, the way performers do when they've clocked a kindness without stopping to thank it properly.
<</link>>

<<link "Listen to the rest before moving on" "Ch1_FestivalStreets_Hub">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
	You stay long enough to hear how it ends -- badly, for the greedy man in the story who tries to steal a piece rather than earn it. The children cheer at his comeuppance with the total lack of mercy only children have for fictional villains.
<</link>>


:: Ch1_FestivalStreets_Weaver {"position":"825,875","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Landmark: Umm Layla's stall
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawWeaver = true>>
<<codex "people" "Umm Layla">>

"Amir!" Umm Layla doesn't look up from the garland she's weaving, fingers moving faster than you could ever follow. "Tell your mother her sugar dough is going to lose to mine again this year, and tell her I said it with love."

She's known you since you were small enough to be underfoot in this exact stall. Half the garlands strung across the street this morning came from her hands, and she'll tell you so, unprompted, at length, if you let her.

<<link "Buy a garland for the doorway at home" "Ch1_FestivalStreets_Hub">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
	<<loseGold 2>>
	<<run $inventory.items.push("Marigold Garland")>>
	She wraps it for you without being asked, the way she's wrapped a hundred others this week, and waves off your thanks like it costs her something to accept it. "Hang it before dusk," she says. "Not after. I don't make them to be forgotten in a bag."
<</link>>

<<link "Admire her work and keep walking" "Ch1_FestivalStreets_Hub">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
	You tell her, honestly, that this year's are her best yet. She finally looks up, pleased despite herself, and pretends the compliment means nothing to her. It very clearly does.
<</link>>


:: Ch1_FestivalStreets_Mosque {"position":"825,975","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Landmark: The Great Mosque
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawMosque = true>>
<<codex "places" "Great Mosque">>

Past the archway, the noise of the street drops away almost entirely. The courtyard is stone-cool underfoot, shaded by an old fig tree that has clearly been arguing with the architecture for longer than the architecture has existed. A few doves pick at nothing in particular near the ablution fountain. Someone has already hung lanterns along the inner colonnade, unlit, waiting like everything else in the city is waiting today.

<<if $personality.faith gte 2>>
	You've come here enough times that the quiet doesn't feel like a pause in your day. It feels like the part of the day that makes the rest of it make sense.
<<else>>
	You don't linger long, but even a short pause here feels like it costs you nothing and gives you something back.
<</if>>

<<link "Sit a while in the quiet" "Ch1_FestivalStreets_Hub">>
	<<personality "faith" 1>>
	You sit on the cool stone until your thoughts stop crowding each other, then rise, unhurried, and go.
<</link>>

<<link "Pay your respects and move on" "Ch1_FestivalStreets_Hub">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	You don't stay long -- there's a whole city out there doing its best to be loud -- but you don't rush the doorway either.
<</link>>


:: Ch1_Yasmina {"position":"625,925","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 4
Yasmina
==================================================*/

<<set $game.location = "Zafirah -- Near the House of Wisdom">>

You cut through the little square outside the House of Wisdom -- not the fastest way to the market, but the quietest, and today quiet is worth the extra distance.

A young woman is sitting on the library steps with a manuscript spread across her knees, so entirely absorbed in it that she hasn't noticed the festival happening around her at all. Ink-stained fingers. A pencil tucked behind one ear that she's clearly forgotten is there. You don't recognize her, but you recognize the posture -- the specific stillness of someone who has forgotten the rest of the world exists.

She mutters something under her breath, traces a symbol in the air above the page with one finger, and--

The corner of the manuscript catches fire. Not dramatically. A small, apologetic little flame, the kind that seems almost embarrassed to be there at all.

"Oh -- no, no, no --" She's patting it out with her bare palm before you've even fully processed what happened, wincing, more annoyed than hurt. A thread of smoke curls up from the singed page.

<<link "Cross over and help her put it out" "Ch1_Yasmina_Help">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
<</link>>

<<link "Call out, half-laughing, that her book is on fire" "Ch1_Yasmina_Joke">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
<</link>>

<<link "Keep walking -- it's clearly under control" "Ch1_Yasmina_Ignore">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch1_Yasmina_Help {"position":"725,925","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Yasmina response: Helped without teasing
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $npc.yasmina.met = true>>
<<relationship "yasmina" 2>>
<<codex "people" "Yasmina">>
<<journal "Met Yasmina outside the House of Wisdom. She'd set her own research on fire.">>

You're across the square before you've decided to be, pressing your sleeve over the last of the smoking corner until it's well and truly out. Up close, the manuscript is old -- older than anything you've handled before -- and covered in a script you don't recognize.

"Thank you." She examines the singed corner with more exasperation than gratitude, like the fire has personally inconvenienced her rather than nearly burned her hand. "That's the third page this week. My father's going to start charging me for the paper."

She looks up at you properly for the first time, sizing you up without any particular subtlety about it.

"You didn't ask what I was doing," she says. "Most people ask what I was doing."

"Should I?"

"No." A faint, genuine smile. "I'm Yasmina. And you have very good reflexes for someone who was just walking past."

<<goto "Ch1_Yasmina_Continue">>


:: Ch1_Yasmina_Joke {"position":"625,1025","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Yasmina response: Joked kindly
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $npc.yasmina.met = true>>
<<relationship "yasmina" 1>>
<<codex "people" "Yasmina">>
<<journal "Met Yasmina outside the House of Wisdom. She'd set her own research on fire.">>

"Your book's on fire," you call out, already crossing toward her, unable to keep the laugh entirely out of your voice.

"I'm aware, thank you." She's patting the last of it out with the flat of her hand, entirely unbothered by your amusement. By the time you reach her the flame's already dead, leaving one blackened corner and a thin thread of smoke. "It does this. Occasionally. It's a *very* minor ward. Mostly."

"Mostly."

"Mostly." She looks up at you, unoffended, faintly amused herself now. "I'm Yasmina. You're allowed to keep laughing, but I'd point out you didn't actually help."

<<goto "Ch1_Yasmina_Continue">>


:: Ch1_Yasmina_Ignore {"position":"525,1025","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Yasmina response: Walked past
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $npc.yasmina.met = true>>
<<codex "people" "Yasmina">>
<<journal "Passed a young woman outside the House of Wisdom whose research had briefly caught fire.">>

She has it out before you've even fully stopped walking -- competent, clearly used to this -- and by the time you glance back she's already blowing the last of the smoke off the page, entirely unbothered.

You keep going. It's not your fire to put out, and the market is waiting.

Behind you, you hear her mutter, to no one in particular, "Yes, please, don't trouble yourself."

<<goto "Ch1_Yasmina_Continue">>


:: Ch1_Yasmina_Continue {"position":"675,1075","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 4 (cont.)
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<if $npc.yasmina.relationship gte 2>>
	"I should get back to this," Yasmina says, already looking down at the manuscript again, though she says it like it costs her something. "My father will want to know if I've found anything before midday. Hakim Nadir -- the scholar. You might know the name." A pause, and then, almost reluctantly: "It was good to meet you, Amir."

	You didn't tell her your name.

	"Small city," she says, before you can ask, and goes back to her ruined page with the faintest smile still on her face.
<<elseif $npc.yasmina.relationship gte 1>>
	"I should get back to this," she says, already looking down at the manuscript again. "My father's expecting results, not smoke stains." She glances up once more. "I'm sure I'll see you around. It's a small city."
<<else>>
	She's already absorbed back into the manuscript by the time you're a few steps away, the small fire apparently the most interesting thing that's happened to her all morning -- and, judging by her focus, already forgotten.
<</if>>

You leave her to it. Ahead, the noise of the Grand Marketplace has grown from a hum into something closer to a roar.

<<link "Head into the marketplace" "Ch1_Market">><</link>>


:: Ch1_Market {"position":"725,1075","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 5
The Marketplace
==================================================*/

<<set $game.location = "Grand Marketplace">>
<<set $places.market.visited = true>>

The Grand Marketplace doesn't so much begin as ambush you.

One step you're in a street; the next, color and noise and smell close over your head like water. Bolts of dyed silk hang from every awning -- saffron, indigo, a red so deep it looks black in shadow. Spice merchants call out prices in voices pitched to carry over a hundred competing voices. Somewhere, badly, someone is still tuning that drum. Underneath everything is the smell of cardamom and hot metal and, faint but constant, the river a few streets beyond.

This is the market you've known your whole life -- not as an idea, but as a hundred small, specific things: the gap in the fifth cobblestone from the fountain that's tripped you since you were six, the exact spot where the fabric merchants' shade cools the air by several degrees, the fig seller who has called you "boy" for as long as she's been alive.

<<goto "Ch1_Market_Hub">>


:: Ch1_Market_Hub {"position":"825,1075","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 5 (cont.)
Marketplace Hub
==================================================*/

<<set _visitedAll = ($choices.sawSpiceMerchant and $choices.sawLanterns and $choices.sawGuardCaptain)>>

<<if !$choices.marketHubSeen>>
	<<set $choices.marketHubSeen = true>>
	There's no reason to rush. Whatever the festival has planned for tonight, the morning still belongs to ordinary things.
<<else>>
	You take another moment before moving on.
<</if>>

<<if !$choices.sawSpiceMerchant>>
* <<link "The spice merchant's stall, a small mountain of color" "Ch1_Market_Spices">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawLanterns>>
* <<link "A cluster of poor-quarter children admiring donated lanterns" "Ch1_Market_Lanterns">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawGuardCaptain>>
* <<link "A guard captain keeping half an eye on the crowd" "Ch1_Market_Guard">><</link>>
<</if>>

<<if _visitedAll>>
	You've made your rounds. The market has given you what it has to give this morning.

	<<link "Head toward the festival ceremony" "Ch1_Ceremony">><</link>>
<<else>>

	<<link "Move on toward the festival ceremony" "Ch1_Ceremony">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: Ch1_Market_Spices {"position":"925,1025","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Landmark: Spice Merchant
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawSpiceMerchant = true>>
<<codex "places" "Grand Marketplace">>

The fig seller -- who has never once called you by your actual name in your entire life -- waves you over with the particular urgency of someone who wants you to admire something.

"Boy. Smell this." She thrusts a pinch of something dark red under your nose before you can object. "New this season. Caravan from the eastern passes, three weeks late, nearly didn't make it before the roads closed for the season." She says it like gossip, not commerce. "Your mother should buy some before the price finds out how good it is."

<<link "Buy a small pouch for Farah" "Ch1_Market_Hub">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
	<<loseGold 3>>
	She wraps it with theatrical care and refuses to let you pay the price she just quoted, muttering something about family discounts that seem to apply to roughly everyone she likes.
<</link>>

<<link "Ask where the caravan really came from" "Ch1_Market_Hub">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
	She launches into a story about washed-out passes and a bandit scare near the border that may or may not be exaggerated for effect. You suspect it is. You don't tell her that.
<</link>>


:: Ch1_Market_Lanterns {"position":"925,1075","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Landmark: Donated Lanterns
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawLanterns = true>>

A crowd of children from the poor quarter has gathered around a stall handing out plain paper lanterns, free, no coin required. An older woman is distributing them one at a time, checking each child's face like she's trying to memorize it.

"The Vizier's household again," a passerby says to no one in particular, following your glance. "Third year running. Say what you want about the man, he doesn't forget the poor quarter at festival time."

<<if $knowledge.qadirTrusted>>
	It matches everything you've ever heard about Qadir ibn Salim -- brilliant, busy, and apparently never too busy to remember people who have nothing to offer him in return.
<</if>>

A small girl clutching her new lantern nearly walks into you, looks up, decides you're not interesting, and keeps going.

<<link "Continue on" "Ch1_Market_Hub">>
	<<personality "faith" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch1_Market_Guard {"position":"925,1125","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Landmark: Guard Captain
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawGuardCaptain = true>>

A guard captain stands near the fountain, arms crossed, watching the crowd with the particular alertness of someone responsible for it if anything goes wrong. She's older than most of the soldiers you've seen posted around the city, and doesn't share their habit of looking bored.

She notices you noticing her and gives a short, professional nod -- the kind that acknowledges you without inviting conversation.

<<link "Nod back and keep walking" "Ch1_Market_Hub">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	You return the nod. She's already looking past you at the crowd again before you've finished the gesture -- not rude, just thorough.
<</link>>

<<link "Ask if she's expecting trouble today" "Ch1_Market_Hub">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
	"Festival crowds are trouble enough on their own," she says, not unkindly, eyes still moving over the square. "Pickpockets mostly. Enjoy the day. That's an order, if you need one to relax."
<</link>>


:: Ch1_Ceremony {"position":"625,1175","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 6
Festival Ceremony
==================================================*/

<<set $game.location = "Zafirah -- The Great Square">>
<<set $game.time = "Dusk">>

By the time you reach the Great Square, the light has turned the color it only turns once a year -- gold going to violet, the sky itself dressed for the occasion. Thousands of unlit lanterns hang overhead in rows, waiting, and the crowd beneath them has the particular hush of a city about to do something it loves doing together.

A dais has been raised at the square's north end. Sultan Rashid stands upon it in ceremonial white, and even from here you can see he looks less like a ruler performing for a crowd and more like a man genuinely glad to be here.

Beside him, a step back and a step to the side, stands a figure in dark green robes who says nothing at all.

<<if !$npc.qadir.met>>
	<<set $npc.qadir.met = true>>
	You don't know his face yet, but you know the robes, and half the crowd around you is already murmuring the name with the particular reverence people reserve for men they've never had reason to distrust. *Qadir ibn Salim.* Grand Vizier. The man whose household, you now know firsthand, remembers the poor quarter at festival time.

	He doesn't speak. He doesn't need to. The Sultan does the talking, and the Vizier simply watches the crowd with an expression of mild, patient satisfaction -- the look of a man content to let someone else enjoy the credit.
<</if>>

The Sultan raises both hands, and the square falls silent all at once, the way a held breath does.

"Another year," he says, and his voice carries further than seems possible without effort. "Another festival. Another chance to remember that light is worth more when it's shared."

He lowers his hands. Somewhere, a signal passes down an invisible chain of runners and criers, and the first lantern kindles -- then the next, then a hundred at once, then the whole square blooming gold from the top down like the sky catching fire on purpose.

The crowd's cheer is loud enough that you feel it as much as hear it.

<<link "Watch the lanterns catch, one after another" "Ch1_GhulAttack_Round1">>
	<<personality "faith" 1>>
<</link>>

<<link "Scan the crowd instead of the sky" "Ch1_GhulAttack_Round1">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	Old habit. You've never quite trusted a crowd this size to stay this happy without something going wrong -- which is, you'll later think, either very good instinct or very bad luck.
<</link>>


:: Ch1_GhulAttack_Round1 {"position":"725,1175","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 7
Ghūl Attack — Round 1: Who do you save first?
==================================================*/

<<set $combat.active = true>>
<<set $combat.round = 1>>
<<set $world.ghoulAttack = true>>

It comes up through the square like the ground itself has torn -- not from the crowd's edge, but from underneath, from a drainage grate nobody thought to guard. Grey-skinned, wrong-jointed, and fast, faster than anything that size should be. The scream that goes up from the crowd isn't festival noise anymore.

You have seconds, not minutes, and three people in the wreckage of the nearest stalls all need you at once:

A spice merchant, pinned beneath a collapsed awning pole, more frightened than hurt but unable to move.

A small child, separated from whoever she came with, frozen directly in the ghūl's path.

A young guardsman, sword already out, but backing away from something that is very clearly winning.

<<link "Go for the child" "Ch1_GhulAttack_Round2">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
	<<reputation "city" 1>>
	<<set $choices.savedFirst = "child">>
	<<journal "A ghūl tore up through the Great Square during the lantern-lighting. I went for the child first.">>
	You don't decide so much as move. You scoop her up mid-stride, and by the time you've set her down behind an overturned cart, someone's arms are already reaching for her -- a mother, sobbing, who was clearly nowhere near close enough to have saved her herself.
<</link>>

<<link "Free the merchant" "Ch1_GhulAttack_Round2">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	<<reputation "city" 1>>
	<<set $choices.savedFirst = "merchant">>
	<<journal "A ghūl tore up through the Great Square during the lantern-lighting. I freed the merchant first.">>
	You get a shoulder under the pole and heave, and the merchant drags himself free a half-second before you'd have had to drop it. "Go," he wheezes, already crawling clear. "Go, go, I'm fine, *go--*"
<</link>>

<<link "Get to the guardsman" "Ch1_GhulAttack_Round2">>
	<<personality "honor" 1>>
	<<reputation "city" 1>>
	<<set $choices.savedFirst = "guard">>
	<<journal "A ghūl tore up through the Great Square during the lantern-lighting. I went to help the guardsman.">>
	He's younger than you expected, and losing badly. You reach him just as his back foot catches on rubble, and you haul him upright by the collar a heartbeat before the ghūl's claws would have found him instead.
<</link>>


:: Ch1_GhulAttack_Round2 {"position":"825,1175","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
SCENE 7 — Round 2: The ghūl closes in
==================================================*/

<<set $combat.round = 2>>

<<if $choices.savedFirst is "child">>
	That leaves the merchant still pinned and the guardsman still losing ground, and the ghūl -- cheated of the one target you took off the board -- pivots toward the nearer of the two.
<<elseif $choices.savedFirst is "merchant">>
	That leaves the child still frozen and the guardsman still losing ground, and the ghūl swings toward them both, momentum barely broken.
<<else>>
	That leaves the merchant still pinned and the child still frozen, and the ghūl, denied its first choice of prey, doesn't hesitate before choosing a second.
<</if>>

Up close, it's worse: too many joints, skin like old leather stretched wrong, a smell like a grave that hasn't been properly closed. It doesn't roar. It just keeps coming, silent, purposeful, hungry in a way that has nothing to do with hunger.

<<link "Attack it head-on" "Ch1_GhulAttack_Round3">>
	<<set $choices.round2Action = "attack">>
	<<personality "honor" 1>>
	<<damage 15>>
	You put yourself between it and the nearest of the two remaining people and drive your blade in hard. It connects -- you feel it connect -- but the thing barely slows, and its answering claw catches your side before you can pull back. It hurts. You stay standing.
<</link>>

<<link "Shield the nearest person instead of striking" "Ch1_GhulAttack_Round3">>
	<<set $choices.round2Action = "protect">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
	<<reputation "city" 1>>
	<<damage 10>>
	<<journal "I chose to shield a stranger from the ghūl rather than strike first.">>
	You get your body and your blade between the ghūl and the person it wants, and you take the blow meant for them across your guard instead. It's a bad trade in every way except the one that matters.
<</link>>

<<link "Hold back and watch how it moves" "Ch1_GhulAttack_Round3">>
	<<set $choices.round2Action = "observe">>
	<<set $combat.observedFire = true>>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
	<<journal "I held back to study the ghūl rather than engage it directly, and noticed it flinch from firelight.">>
	You don't like the decision even as you make it, but you force yourself to watch instead of swing -- and you see it: every time the creature's wild path carries it near one of the lantern braziers still burning at the square's edge, it flinches. Not fear, exactly. Something closer to pain.
<</link>>

<<link "Pull back and shout for the guard" "Ch1_GhulAttack_Round3">>
	<<set $choices.round2Action = "retreat">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	<<set $reputation.city -= 1>>
	<<journal "I fell back and called for help rather than engaging the ghūl myself. Someone else was hurt because of it.">>
	You give ground, shouting for the guard, buying distance instead of spending it. It costs something -- you hear, rather than see, someone else in the crowd cry out as the ghūl's attention finds an easier target for a moment -- but you're still standing, and so, you'll find out shortly, is everyone else.
<</link>>


:: Ch1_GhulAttack_Round3 {"position":"925,1175","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
SCENE 7 — Round 3: The finish
==================================================*/

<<set $combat.round = 3>>

<<if $combat.observedFire>>
	You know what to do now. The nearest brazier is close enough to reach in three strides, if you can make the ghūl follow.

	<<link "Drive it toward the lantern braziers" "Ch1_GhulAttack_Victory">>
		<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
		<<reputation "city" 1>>
		You feint wide, drawing its lunge toward the burning brazier rather than away from it. The moment its shoulder brushes the flame it recoils -- a full, violent flinch, the closest thing to a sound it's made all fight -- and in that half-second of confusion you put your blade where the flinch left it undefended. It goes down hard, folding in on itself like something that was never meant to hold a shape for long.
	<</link>>
<<else>>
	There's no clever option left, only the straightforward one. It's flagging -- slower now than it was, whether from the fight or something else -- and you won't get a better opening than this.

	<<link "Finish it" "Ch1_GhulAttack_Victory">>
		<<personality "honor" 1>>
		<<damage 20>>
		You put everything you have left into it, and it's enough, but only just. The blade goes in to the hilt, and the creature collapses, folding in on itself like something that was never meant to hold a shape for long. You're breathing hard. Your side is bleeding freely. You're alive.
	<</link>>
<</if>>


:: Ch1_GhulAttack_Victory {"position":"1025,1175","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
SCENE 7 (end) — Aftermath of combat
==================================================*/

<<set $combat.active = false>>
<<set $combat.victory = true>>

Where the ghūl fell, there's no body -- only a scatter of grey ash already losing its shape in the evening wind, and a scorched, uneven ring burned into the flagstones beneath it.

Around you, the square is finding its feet again: someone is crying, someone else is laughing in the specific unhinged way people laugh right after they were nearly not laughing ever again, and the lanterns overhead are still burning, undisturbed, as if they hadn't noticed anything had happened at all.

<<if $player.health lte 50>>
	Your side throbs with every breath. Someone presses a cloth to it before you can object. You'll live. It'll scar.
<</if>>

<<link "Take stock of the square" "Ch1_Aftermath">><</link>>


:: Ch1_Aftermath {"position":"1125,1175","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 8
Aftermath
==================================================*/

<<set $game.location = "Zafirah -- The Great Square">>
<<set $world.citySafe = true>>

The square survives. That's the thing you keep returning to as the immediate panic settles into something quieter -- torn awnings, a few injuries, nothing that won't heal, nothing that will be remembered as anything worse than the year the festival went wrong. The lanterns are still lit. Someone has already started stacking the broken stall timber into a neat pile, because that is, apparently, simply what people do.

You're still catching your breath when Yasmina finds you, manuscript forgotten, staring at the scorched ring on the flagstones with an expression that has nothing casual left in it.

"Amir." She crouches beside the mark, tracing its edge without touching it. "Look at this."

Burned into the stone, half-obscured by ash, is a symbol -- angular, deliberate, nothing like the scorch pattern a dying creature should leave by accident. A circle broken in seven places.

"That's not damage," she says quietly. "That's *writing.*"

<<if $choices.tookCompass>>
	Without quite meaning to, your hand goes to your pocket, to the compass that has never once pointed north. You don't say anything. Neither does she -- yet.
<</if>>

"I don't recognize it," Yasmina admits, straightening. "And I've spent three years reading things most people in this city don't know exist. That should worry you as much as it worries me."

<<link "Ask her what she thinks it means" "Ch1_Investigation">>
	<<relationship "yasmina" 1>>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
<</link>>

<<link "Suggest she's imagining a pattern in ash and soot" "Ch1_Investigation">>
	<<relationship "yasmina" -1>>
	"It's ash, Yasmina. Ash doesn't write."

	She gives you a long, unimpressed look. "Neither do ghūls, ordinarily. And yet."
<</link>>


:: Ch1_Investigation {"position":"1225,1175","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 1 — SCENE 9
Investigation Begins
==================================================*/

<<set $game.location = "House of Wisdom">>
<<set $game.time = "Night">>

Hakim Nadir is exactly what you expected of a man whose daughter sets her own research on fire out of enthusiasm: sharp-eyed, half-buried in books, and entirely unsurprised to see his daughter dragging a bruised young man into his study well past a reasonable hour.

<<set $npc.hakim.met = true>>
<<codex "people" "Hakim Nadir">>

He studies the sketch Yasmina hurriedly copied from the flagstone for a long moment before he says anything at all.

"A broken circle. Seven divisions." He sets it down like it might still be hot. "I've seen this mark exactly once before, in a manuscript so old the binding had turned to dust. It was described as the ward-sign of a *guardian* -- one of several, if the text is to be believed, that once stood watch over something the old scholars refused to name directly."

"Refused, or couldn't?" Yasmina asks.

"With scholars, those are rarely different things." Hakim looks up at Amir properly for the first time. "Whatever tore that symbol into the stone tonight was not simply a wild creature that wandered into a festival, young man. Something disturbed it. Or someone."

<<objective "Investigate the Shrine of the First Guardian">>
<<set $knowledge.knowsSevenSeals = false>>
<<journal "Hakim Nadir believes the symbol marks a forgotten guardian. He wants to research further before we act.">>

"I need time with this properly," Hakim says, already reaching for a shelf of texts that look like they'd crumble under a careless hand. "Come back tomorrow. Both of you." A pause, and something almost like reluctant approval crosses his face as his eyes move between you and his daughter. "And Amir -- whatever this turns out to be, I suspect the festival is the last quiet night this city has for a while."

Outside, the lanterns are still burning over a city that doesn't yet know how much has changed.

Sleep is a long time coming -- not from fear, but from the feeling, new and strange, that the world has doors in it you never noticed, and one of them has just come open.

<<link "Return to Hakim's study the next morning" "Ch2_HakimStudy">>
	<<journal "Returned to the House of Wisdom the morning after the festival, as Hakim asked.">>
<</link>>

<!-- End of Chapter 1 -->


:: Ch2_HakimStudy {"position":"1225,1275","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 2 — SCENE 1
Hakim's Study
==================================================*/

<<set $game.chapter = 2>>
<<set $game.scene = 1>>
<<set $game.day = 2>>
<<set $game.time = "Morning">>
<<set $game.location = "House of Wisdom">>

Hakim hasn't slept. You can tell by the state of the study, which was merely cluttered last night and has since achieved something closer to archaeological. Yasmina steps over a stack of unbound pages without looking down, clearly used to navigating her father's study by memory rather than sight.

"I found it," Hakim says, without preamble, without looking up. "Or -- a fragment of it. Enough." He taps a brittle page covered in a script even you can tell is very old. "This mark belongs to a tradition of guardian-wards. Ancient. Pre-dating the Sultanate by centuries, possibly longer. Each ward marked a place where something was being *kept.*"

"Kept how? Kept what?" Yasmina leans over his shoulder.

"I think --" she catches herself, "-- I think the text calls it a relic. But the word could mean almost anything. My father would say the same."

Hakim doesn't correct her. That, more than anything, tells you how uncertain even he is.

"There's a shrine," he says instead. "Beyond the northern caravan routes, in the hill country. If this ward-sign is genuine -- and I believe it is -- that shrine may hold answers neither of us can find in a book." He finally looks up at you. "I won't pretend this is safe, Amir. But I don't think it's optional, either. Something disturbed whatever that guardian was keeping. It didn't disturb itself."

<<link "Agree because someone has to answer for what happened in the square" "Ch2_HakimStudy_Continue">>
	<<personality "honor" 1>>
	<<set $choices.whyInvestigate = "honor">>
<</link>>

<<link "Agree because you need to know what that symbol means" "Ch2_HakimStudy_Continue">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
	<<set $choices.whyInvestigate = "curiosity">>
<</link>>

<<link "Agree because if it happens again, more people get hurt" "Ch2_HakimStudy_Continue">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
	<<set $choices.whyInvestigate = "mercy">>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_HakimStudy_Continue {"position":"1325,1275","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Scene 1 (cont.) — Yasmina joins
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<if $choices.whyInvestigate is "honor">>
	"Good," Hakim says simply, as if that settles something he'd privately been unsure of.
<<elseif $choices.whyInvestigate is "curiosity">>
	Hakim's mouth twitches -- not quite a smile. "That's usually how it starts for me too."
<<else>>
	Hakim nods slowly. "Then we understand each other."
<</if>>

Yasmina straightens, and there's a particular set to her shoulders that you're beginning to recognize as *decided.*

"I'm going with him."

Hakim's head comes up sharply. "Yasmina --"

"You can't read half of what we might find out there, and neither can he." She says it plainly, not unkindly, the way you state a fact rather than win an argument. "I can. That's not a request, Father. It's a reason."

The silence that follows is long enough that you start to wonder if you should say something. You don't get the chance.

"You'll take the sealed satchel," Hakim says finally, quiet, resigned in the specific way of a man who has lost this exact argument before and knows better than to pretend otherwise this time. "And you'll listen to him when it matters." A glance at you. "And you'll listen to *her* when it matters more."

<<run $party.push("yasmina")>>
<<journal "Yasmina is coming with me to the shrine. Her father agreed, reluctantly.">>

<<link "Head home to prepare" "Ch2_Departure">><</link>>


:: Ch2_Departure {"position":"1225,1375","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 2 — SCENE 2
Departure
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 2>>
<<set $game.location = "Amir's Home">>

The city outside looks almost ordinary in daylight -- awnings re-strung, ash swept from the square, a few too many people finding reasons to walk past the scorched flagstones without quite looking at them. Zafirah is doing what Zafirah always does after anything: continuing, loudly, as if that itself were an answer to what happened.

Farah doesn't cry when you tell her. You'd almost rather she did. Instead she goes very quiet, then very practical, packing a satchel with more bread than one person could reasonably eat in a week.

"Your father used to say the road takes longer than you plan and shorter than you fear." She ties the satchel closed with more force than it requires. "I never knew what he meant by that. I still don't. Take it anyway."

<<if $choices.tookCompass>>
	Her eyes flick, just once, to your pocket. She doesn't say anything about it. You don't offer anything either.
<</if>>

<<link "Embrace her before you go" "Ch2_Departure_Continue">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
	You hold on a moment longer than either of you is used to. Neither of you comments on it.
<</link>>

<<link "Promise her you'll be careful" "Ch2_Departure_Continue">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	"I know that face," she says. "It's the one your father made right before he was about to be reckless. Prove me wrong."
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Departure_Continue {"position":"1325,1375","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Scene 2 (cont.) — Leaving the city
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $inventory.items.push("Traveling Rations")>>
<<journal "Said goodbye to my mother. Left Zafirah with Yasmina to find the Shrine of the First Guardian.">>

The northern gate is busier than usual -- festival traffic still clearing out, caravan masters shouting manifests over each other, a general sense of a city exhaling after holding its breath too long. Yasmina is waiting for you near the gate with a satchel nearly as large as she is and an expression that suggests she has already read three-quarters of whatever's in it.

"Ready?" she asks, in the tone of someone who has already decided the answer doesn't matter.

<<link "Yes" "Ch2_Caravan">><</link>>


:: Ch2_Caravan {"position":"1225,1475","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 2 — SCENE 3
The Caravan
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 3>>
<<set $game.location = "The Northern Road">>
<<set $game.day = 3>>

The caravan is larger than you expected -- a dozen wagons, twice that many camels, pilgrims and merchants and at least one family who seem to be moving their entire household somewhere for reasons nobody's asked about yet. The caravan master, a broad woman named Sabiha with sun-cracked hands and a voice built for shouting over wind, checks your names against a manifest without much interest and waves you toward the rear wagons.

"Northern route's diverted a few days out," she adds, almost as an afterthought, already turning to the next name on her list. "Bridge trouble past Wadi Nur. Some official recommended the longer pass through the hill road instead -- safer this time of year, apparently, and the paperwork was already stamped by the time it reached me, so." She shrugs. "Longer, but safer. No complaints from me."

<<if $choices.tookCompass>>
	In your pocket, for just a moment, the compass feels colder than the morning air accounts for. You don't mention it. You're not sure what you'd say.
<</if>>

The wagons roll out at a pace that feels, after the last two days, almost insultingly calm.

<<goto "Ch2_Caravan_Hub">>


:: Ch2_Caravan_Hub {"position":"1325,1475","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 2 — SCENE 3 (cont.)
Caravan Hub
==================================================*/

<<if !$choices.caravanHubSeen>>
	<<set $choices.caravanHubSeen = true>>
	Days on the road settle into their own rhythm quickly. There's time, now, in a way there wasn't in Zafirah.
<<else>>
	The road rolls on beneath you, unhurried.
<</if>>

<<if !$choices.metSabiha>>
* <<link "Talk with Sabiha as she walks the caravan's length" "Ch2_Caravan_Sabiha">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.metPilgrim>>
* <<link "An old pilgrim traveling alone shares your fire one evening" "Ch2_Caravan_Pilgrim">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.talkedYasmina>>
* <<link "Yasmina has been quiet, reading, most of the journey" "Ch2_Caravan_Yasmina">><</link>>
<</if>>

<<if $choices.metSabiha and $choices.metPilgrim and $choices.talkedYasmina>>
	Ahead, the hill road climbs out of the flatlands, and the caravan's easy pace starts, gradually, to change.

	<<link "Continue toward the hills" "Ch2_Caravan_End">><</link>>
<<else>>

	<<link "Let the days pass and continue toward the hills" "Ch2_Caravan_End">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: Ch2_Caravan_Sabiha {"position":"1425,1425","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Caravan: Sabiha, the caravan master
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.metSabiha = true>>
<<codex "people" "Sabiha">>

"Twenty-two years on these roads," Sabiha tells you, unprompted, swinging up onto the lead wagon's bench beside you like she's done it a thousand times, because she has. "You learn to read a caravan the way a sailor reads weather. Who's going to cause trouble. Who's going to need help before they ask for it. Who's running from something."

She looks at you sideways, not unkindly.

"You're not running from anything. You're walking toward something. Different look entirely." She doesn't ask what. You get the sense she's learned not to.

<<link "Ask about caravan life" "Ch2_Caravan_Hub">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
	She talks, easily and at length, about hospitality-law older than the Sultanate -- that any traveler who shares your fire is owed protection until dawn, no exceptions, no questions. "Break that," she says, "and no caravan in the north will carry you again. Some laws don't need soldiers to enforce them."
<</link>>

<<link "Ask if she trusts the diverted route" "Ch2_Caravan_Hub">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	She shrugs, easy and unbothered. "Paperwork's paperwork. Bridges wash out every flood season, and someone in the palace always finds a safer road to stamp in its place. Long as we make good time, I don't ask which clerk did the stamping."
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Caravan_Pilgrim {"position":"1425,1475","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Caravan: The old pilgrim
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.metPilgrim = true>>

He doesn't offer his name, and you don't ask -- pilgrims on the northern road rarely do, Sabiha told you, as if the walking itself is meant to matter more than the person doing it. He shares dried dates from a pouch that looks nearly empty and refuses to hear a word about you not taking your share.

"Where I'm walking, it doesn't matter if I arrive with anything," he says, when you ask. "Only that I arrive changed." He smiles at your expression. "You'll understand that better by the time your own road ends. Or you won't. Both are common enough."

<<link "Ask what changed him" "Ch2_Caravan_Hub">>
	<<personality "faith" 1>>
	He's quiet long enough that you think he won't answer. "Grief," he says eventually. "Then, much later, gratitude for having survived it. In that order. Never the other way, no matter what the storytellers promise you."
<</link>>

<<link "Offer him more of your own supplies" "Ch2_Caravan_Hub">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
	<<loseGold 2>>
	He accepts with a small bow of the head, no false modesty about it. "Hospitality repaid is hospitality understood," he says, which you suspect is a proverb, though you've never heard it before.
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Caravan_Yasmina {"position":"1425,1525","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Caravan: Yasmina, on the road
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.talkedYasmina = true>>

You find her cross-legged atop a supply crate, the sealed satchel open across her lap, frowning at a page like it's personally wronged her.

"I think this passage describes a trial," she says, without looking up, the way people do when they've been alone with their own thoughts long enough to forget anyone might not already be following them. "Or a warning. Possibly both. The grammar's archaic enough that I'm working from context more than certainty."

"You don't know?"

"I know *fragments.*" She says it without embarrassment, closing the page carefully. "My father spent thirty years on texts like this and still calls himself a student. I'd rather tell you 'I think' and be right twice as often as tell you 'I know' and be wrong once where it costs someone something."

<<link "Tell her that's more honest than most scholars manage" "Ch2_Caravan_Hub">>
	<<personality "honor" 1>>
	<<relationship "yasmina" 2>>
	Something in her expression eases, just slightly -- the look of someone braced for a dismissal that didn't come. "Most people want certainty," she says. "I've never been able to give it to them honestly. I'm glad you didn't ask me to start now."
<</link>>

<<link "Ask her to read some of it aloud, uncertain or not" "Ch2_Caravan_Hub">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
	<<relationship "yasmina" 1>>
	She does, slowly, translating half a line at a time, second-guessing herself twice and correcting once. It's slow going. You don't mind. Neither, you suspect, does she.
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Caravan_End {"position":"1325,1575","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 2 — SCENE 3 (end)
Into the Hills
==================================================*/

<<set $game.location = "The Hill Road">>

The flatlands give way to stone and scrub, and the caravan's pace slows to match the climbing ground. Sabiha calls back that your party's turn-off is close now -- the shrine road properly begins where the caravan route ends, a day's walk further than any wagon will comfortably go.

Somewhere behind you, already receding, is every part of your life that made sense without needing an explanation.

<<link "Leave the caravan and continue on foot" "Ch2_Desert">>
	<<journal "Left the caravan at the hill road. The shrine is a day's walk from here.">>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Desert {"position":"1225,1675","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 2 — SCENE 4
The Empty Desert
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 4>>
<<set $game.location = "The Northern Desert">>
<<set $game.day = 4>>
<<set $quest.main = "Find the Shrine of the First Guardian">>

The caravan doesn't so much stop as thin out around you until it's gone, wagon wheels and camel bells fading into a quiet that has weight to it. What's left is an old stone marker, waist-high, worn nearly featureless by centuries of wind, and beyond it nothing but dunes in every direction that matters.

The driver who peels off to set you down doesn't linger. He barely looks at the marker at all -- the look of a man who's done this before and stopped being curious about it a long time ago.

"If the Guardian wishes to meet you, you will," he says, already turning his camel back toward the road.

"That's it?" Yasmina asks. "No path? No landmark?"

He doesn't answer. He's already three lengths gone, and by the time you think to call after him, he's little more than a shape in the heat-shimmer, and then not even that.

The silence that's left behind doesn't feel empty. It feels like it's waiting for something.

<<goto "Ch2_Desert_Hub">>


:: Ch2_Desert_Hub {"position":"1325,1675","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 2 — SCENE 4 (cont.)
Desert Hub
==================================================*/

<<set _visitedAll = ($choices.sawDesertStones and $choices.sawDesertCarvings and $choices.sawDesertCampsite and $choices.sawDesertWell)>>

<<if !$choices.desertHubSeen>>
	<<set $choices.desertHubSeen = true>>
	There's no obvious direction to walk in, so you let curiosity choose for you.
<<else>>
	You look around a while longer before deciding.
<</if>>

<<if !$choices.sawDesertStones>>
* <<link "A scatter of wind-worn stones, oddly placed" "Ch2_Desert_Stones">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawDesertCarvings>>
* <<link "Faded carvings on the marker itself" "Ch2_Desert_Carvings">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawDesertCampsite>>
* <<link "An old, abandoned campsite half-buried nearby" "Ch2_Desert_Campsite">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawDesertWell>>
* <<link "A dried well, its stones sun-bleached white" "Ch2_Desert_Well">><</link>>
<</if>>

<<if _visitedAll>>
	You've looked as far as looking will get you. Whatever happens next isn't going to announce itself from a hole in the ground.
<</if>>

<<link "Walk on into the dunes" "Ch2_Shrine_Appears">><</link>>


:: Ch2_Desert_Stones {"position":"1425,1625","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Landmark: Wind-worn stones
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawDesertStones = true>>
<<codex "lore" "The Waymarker Stones">>

A dozen stones, no two alike, are set in a loose ring some distance from the marker -- too deliberate to be natural, too worn to have been placed recently. When you crouch beside the nearest one, you can just make out a shape ground into its face, smoothed almost flat by wind and time.

It might be a hand. It might be nothing at all.

<<link "Move on" "Ch2_Desert_Hub">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Desert_Carvings {"position":"1425,1675","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Landmark: Faded carvings
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawDesertCarvings = true>>
<<codex "lore" "The Marker's Carvings">>

Up close, the stone marker isn't as featureless as it looked from a distance. Faint lines run its length, a script you don't recognize, worn down to shadow rather than shape. You trace one groove with a fingertip and can't tell if you're reading a warning or a welcome.

<<if $choices.tookCompass>>
	The compass in your pocket sits very still. Stiller than it's sat in days.
<</if>>

<<link "Move on" "Ch2_Desert_Hub">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Desert_Campsite {"position":"1425,1725","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Landmark: Abandoned campsite
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawDesertCampsite = true>>
<<codex "lore" "The Abandoned Camp">>

The remains of a fire ring, long cold, sit half-swallowed by drifted sand. Whoever camped here left in a hurry, or didn't leave at all -- there's a waterskin, cracked and empty, and a single sandal too small for an adult's foot.

Yasmina crouches beside it a long moment, saying nothing, and doesn't offer a theory. Neither do you.

<<link "Move on" "Ch2_Desert_Hub">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Desert_Well {"position":"1425,1775","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Landmark: Dried well
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawDesertWell = true>>
<<codex "lore" "The Dry Well">>

The well's stones have been bleached the color of bone, and whatever water it once held is long gone, leaving only a dark throat that swallows the light a few feet down. You drop a pebble in out of habit more than curiosity. You don't hear it land.

"People don't build wells in places that were never going to need one," Yasmina says quietly. "Something lived out here. Once."

<<link "Move on" "Ch2_Desert_Hub">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Shrine_Appears {"position":"1325,1775","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 2 — SCENE 5
The Shrine Appears
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 5>>

You walk until the marker is a memory behind you and the dunes ahead look exactly like the dunes you've already crossed -- and then, without any particular moment you could point to, the wind simply stops.

Not dies down. Stops, all at once, the way a held breath does.

The sand around your feet begins to settle, grain by grain, in a silence so complete you can hear your own pulse. And where a moment ago there was only slope and shadow, a staircase is emerging from beneath the dune -- old stone, one step at a time, as the wind that's held it hidden for who knows how long simply lets it go.

"It was always there," Yasmina breathes, staring. "We just couldn't see it."

<<set $places.shrine.visited = true>>
<<set $game.location = "The First Shrine">>
<<codex "places" "Shrine of the First Guardian">>
<<journal "The desert wind died without warning, and a stone staircase rose out of the sand -- the First Shrine, hidden until it chose to be found.">>

At the bottom of the stairs, modest and unadorned, is a doorway with no door in it. No treasure glints from within. No grandeur announces itself. Only age, patient and unhurried, the way only very old things are.

<<link "Descend the staircase" "Ch2_Guardian_Basim">><</link>>


:: Ch2_Guardian_Basim {"position":"1325,1875","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 2 — SCENE 6
The First Guardian
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 6>>

The doorway opens onto a single bare chamber, cool and dim, and an old man is already waiting there as if he'd been expecting you since long before you arrived. He's dressed in simple white robes, a wooden staff in one hand worn smooth by decades of the same grip, and he doesn't turn toward the sound of your footsteps so much as turn toward *you*, specifically, the way a person does when they already know exactly who's walked in.

His eyes are clouded pale with age. He is, you realize a half-second later, entirely blind.

"Amir." He says it plainly, warmly, the way you'd greet someone you'd been waiting on. Not a guess. Not a question.

You didn't tell him your name. Neither, you're fairly sure, did anyone else.

<<set $npc.basim.met = true>>
<<codex "people" "Sheikh Basim">>
<<journal "Met Sheikh Basim, the First Guardian. He knew my name before I gave it.">>

"How do you know that?" Yasmina asks, before you can.

Basim's smile doesn't waver. "The same way I knew you'd ask." He says it without mischief, the way you'd state a fact about the weather. "I was told you were coming, child. Names travel faster than feet, out here, if you know how to listen for them."

<<link "Ask how long he's known" "Ch2_Guardian_Basim_Talk">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
	"How long have you known I was coming?" you ask, more startled than you'd like to admit.
<</link>>

<<link "Ask what he is" "Ch2_Guardian_Basim_Talk">>
	<<personality "honor" 1>>
	"What are you?" you ask -- not unkindly, but plainly, the way you'd ask a soldier his rank.
<</link>>

<<link "Simply greet him in return" "Ch2_Guardian_Basim_Talk">>
	<<personality "faith" 1>>
	You incline your head instead of asking anything at all. Whatever he is, he clearly means no harm, and some things don't need an explanation before they're offered respect.
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Guardian_Basim_Talk {"position":"1425,1875","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Scene 6 (cont.) — Basim explains himself
--------------------------------------------------*/

"Long enough that I've stopped counting in years," Basim says, settling onto a low stone bench with the unhurried care of someone who trusts his own balance completely. "I keep watch here. That's the whole of it. Not a riddle -- simply a very long, very quiet job."

"Keep watch over what?" Yasmina asks, glancing around the bare chamber. "There's nothing here."

"There is exactly what there needs to be." He tilts his head toward Amir, unerringly, though there's nothing in the room to have told him where you were standing. "You've come a long way to ask a stone a question it isn't going to answer for you. I can offer something better, if you're willing."

"What's that?" you ask.

"A door." He rises, staff tapping once against the floor. "Whether you walk through it is entirely your own affair."

<<link "Follow him" "Ch2_Trial_Enter">><</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Enter {"position":"1425,1975","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 2 — SCENE 7
The Trial Begins
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 7>>
<<set $trial.active = true>>
<<set $trial.progress = 0>>
<<set $quest.main = "Complete the First Guardian's trial">>

<<if $trial.attempts is 0>>
	Basim leads you to the back of the chamber, where the stone gives way to a passage you'd have sworn wasn't there a moment ago -- low, dark, and cool with air that's been trapped underground for longer than your whole life.

	"There is no trick to this," he says, before you can ask. "No riddle waiting in the dark to trip you. Only the walking, and whatever you decide to do with it." He pauses at the threshold. "You may stop at any point. That is not failure. That is simply stopping."
<<else>>
	Basim leads you back to the same dark passage without comment, the way you'd return to an unfinished conversation. "Whenever you're ready," is all he says.
<</if>>

Yasmina falls into step just behind you, satchel left topside, her expression somewhere between curious and unreadable.

<<link "Enter the passage" "Ch2_Trial_Passage1">><</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Passage1 {"position":"1525,1975","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
SCENE 7 (cont.) — The Collapsed Corridor
==================================================*/

<<if !$choices.trialPassage1Seen>>
	<<set $choices.trialPassage1Seen = true>>
	The passage narrows fast, and twenty paces in you meet the first collapse -- old stone fallen from the ceiling in a heap you have to climb rather than walk over. Basim goes around it by a route he clearly doesn't need his eyes to know, staff tapping a steady rhythm against the rubble.

	"We can turn back here, if you'd like," he says, not unkindly, not testing you with it. "There's no shame in a short walk."
<<else>>
	You're back at the collapsed corridor. It hasn't gotten any easier to look at.
<</if>>

<<if !$choices.sawTrialObserve1>>
* <<link "Look closer at the fallen stone" "Ch2_Trial_Passage1_Observe">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawTrialYasmina1>>
* <<link "Check on Yasmina" "Ch2_Trial_Passage1_Yasmina">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawTrialBasim1>>
* <<link "Ask Basim something" "Ch2_Trial_Passage1_Basim">><</link>>
<</if>>

<<link "Continue deeper" "Ch2_Trial_Passage2">>
	<<set $trial.progress += 1>>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
<</link>>

<<link "Turn back" "Ch2_Trial_Retreat">><</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Passage1_Observe {"position":"1625,1925","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Trial Passage 1: Observe
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawTrialObserve1 = true>>

The fallen stone isn't random rubble -- you can make out cut edges under the dust, blocks that were shaped by hands once, long before they were shaped by collapse. Someone built this corridor to be walked, not to be broken. Something else did the breaking.

<<link "Return to the passage" "Ch2_Trial_Passage1">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Passage1_Yasmina {"position":"1625,1975","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Trial Passage 1: Yasmina
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawTrialYasmina1 = true>>

"You don't have to do this too," you tell her, low enough that Basim, ahead, may or may not hear it.

"I know." She doesn't stop climbing over the rubble. "I'm not doing it for you. I'm doing it because I'd rather see it than read about it later." A faint, dust-streaked smile. "Don't let it go to your head."

<<link "Return to the passage" "Ch2_Trial_Passage1">>
	<<relationship "yasmina" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Passage1_Basim {"position":"1625,2025","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Trial Passage 1: Ask Basim
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawTrialBasim1 = true>>

"How many people turn back?" you ask him.

"More than you'd think. Fewer than you'd fear." He says it without judgment either way, the words as level as the ground he's walking despite never once looking down at it. "The ones who turn back are not the ones I worry for."

<<link "Return to the passage" "Ch2_Trial_Passage1">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Passage2 {"position":"1525,2025","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
SCENE 7 (cont.) — The Steep Climb
==================================================*/

<<if !$choices.trialPassage2Seen>>
	<<set $choices.trialPassage2Seen = true>>
	The passage tilts upward and keeps tilting, until you're climbing more than walking, hands finding holds in stone worn smooth by centuries of other hands doing exactly the same thing. Your legs are starting to feel it. So, you notice, is your breathing.

	"We could return tomorrow," Basim offers, from somewhere just ahead, unbothered by the climb in a way that seems faintly unfair. "The stairs will still be here."
<<else>>
	The climb is exactly as steep the second time.
<</if>>

<<if !$choices.sawTrialObserve2>>
* <<link "Look for handholds worn deliberately, not by accident" "Ch2_Trial_Passage2_Observe">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawTrialYasmina2>>
* <<link "Check on Yasmina" "Ch2_Trial_Passage2_Yasmina">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawTrialBasim2>>
* <<link "Ask Basim something" "Ch2_Trial_Passage2_Basim">><</link>>
<</if>>

<<link "Continue climbing" "Ch2_Trial_Passage3">>
	<<set $trial.progress += 1>>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
<</link>>

<<link "Turn back" "Ch2_Trial_Retreat">><</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Passage2_Observe {"position":"1625,2075","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Trial Passage 2: Observe
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawTrialObserve2 = true>>

Where you'd expect natural rock, your fingers keep finding shallow grooves instead -- handholds, placed with intent, spaced for a stride shorter than your own. Someone smaller than you climbed this before you did. More than once, from the wear.

<<link "Return to the climb" "Ch2_Trial_Passage2">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Passage2_Yasmina {"position":"1625,2125","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Trial Passage 2: Yasmina
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawTrialYasmina2 = true>>

"You're breathing like you're dying," you tell her, only half joking, offering a hand up the next ledge.

"I'm reading and climbing at the same time," she says, waving off the hand and taking the ledge herself, satchel-less and stubborn. "Forgive me for not making it look easy." But she takes the next handhold you point out without arguing, which is its own kind of answer.

<<link "Return to the climb" "Ch2_Trial_Passage2">>
	<<relationship "yasmina" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Passage2_Basim {"position":"1625,2175","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Trial Passage 2: Ask Basim
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawTrialBasim2 = true>>

"Doesn't this ever get easier for you?" you ask, watching him take the climb without a single misstep.

"No," he says, simply, sounding almost pleased about it. "But I stopped needing it to be easy a long time ago. That's not the same as it being hard, either. It's just the shape of the road."

<<link "Return to the climb" "Ch2_Trial_Passage2">>
	<<personality "faith" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Passage3 {"position":"1525,2075","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
SCENE 7 (cont.) — The Narrow Ledge
==================================================*/

<<if !$choices.trialPassage3Seen>>
	<<set $choices.trialPassage3Seen = true>>
	The last light from the passage behind you gives out entirely, and what's left is a ledge barely wider than your own two feet, running along a drop you can hear more than see -- a faint, distant echo when a loose pebble goes over the edge. Your legs ache. Your hands are scraped raw. The dark presses close enough to feel like a hand at your back.

	"This is usually where people stop," Basim says, voice steady and close in the blackness. "Not because they can't go on. Because they've run out of reasons to believe there's anything left to reach."
<<else>>
	The ledge is no kinder the second time through, and no shorter for the familiarity.
<</if>>

<<if !$choices.sawTrialObserve3>>
* <<link "Listen to the dark instead of fighting it" "Ch2_Trial_Passage3_Observe">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawTrialYasmina3>>
* <<link "Check on Yasmina" "Ch2_Trial_Passage3_Yasmina">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawTrialBasim3>>
* <<link "Ask Basim something" "Ch2_Trial_Passage3_Basim">><</link>>
<</if>>

<<link "Continue along the ledge" "Ch2_Trial_Threshold">>
	<<set $trial.progress += 1>>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
<</link>>

<<link "Turn back" "Ch2_Trial_Retreat">><</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Passage3_Observe {"position":"1625,2225","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Trial Passage 3: Observe
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawTrialObserve3 = true>>

You stop fighting the dark and let your other senses do the work instead -- the dry, mineral smell of very old stone, the faint draft that means the passage keeps going somewhere, the sound of your own footsteps changing pitch as the walls narrow and widen around you. It doesn't make the ledge less narrow. It makes it less frightening.

<<link "Return to the ledge" "Ch2_Trial_Passage3">>
	<<personality "faith" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Passage3_Yasmina {"position":"1625,2275","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Trial Passage 3: Yasmina
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawTrialYasmina3 = true>>

"Talk to me," you say into the dark, and hear her exhale, half a laugh, entirely relieved to be asked.

"I'm terrified," she admits, plainly, no performance in it at all. "I don't like not being able to read anything. I don't like not knowing how far down that drop is." A pause, footsteps steadying. "I'm still walking, though. Don't let that go to your head either."

<<link "Return to the ledge" "Ch2_Trial_Passage3">>
	<<relationship "yasmina" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Passage3_Basim {"position":"1625,2325","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Trial Passage 3: Ask Basim
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawTrialBasim3 = true>>

"What's waiting at the end of this?" you ask.

"I don't know," Basim says, and it sounds like the truth, plain and unadorned. "I've never walked it as the one being tested. Only as the one walking beside." A beat. "I can tell you what it isn't, if that helps. It isn't a trap. Whatever you find, you'll have earned the finding of it."

<<link "Return to the ledge" "Ch2_Trial_Passage3">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Retreat {"position":"1525,2375","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
SCENE 7 (cont.) — Turning Back
==================================================*/

<<set $trial.active = false>>
<<set $trial.progress = 0>>
<<set $trial.attempts += 1>>

You stop. Somewhere ahead, Basim's steady footsteps stop too, without a trace of disappointment in them.

"That's enough for today," he says, turning back the way you came as easily as if he'd planned to all along. "There's no debt in this. Rest. Eat something. The passage isn't going anywhere."

Yasmina doesn't argue, and doesn't look at you like you've failed at anything, which somehow makes it easier to consider trying again.

<<link "Rest, then try again" "Ch2_Trial_Enter">><</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Threshold {"position":"1525,2425","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
SCENE 7 (end) — The Final Chamber's Threshold
==================================================*/

The ledge finally widens into a landing, and ahead of you the dark gives way to the faint outline of a doorway -- the last one, you're certain of it, though you couldn't say how you know.

Basim stops. Yasmina, a step behind him, stops too, and something in the stillness tells you this isn't her choice to make.

"This road belongs to him," Basim says gently, turning his clouded eyes toward her anyway, the way he seems to see everyone regardless. "You've carried him well this far. The rest of it, he carries alone."

Yasmina looks at you for a long moment. Whatever she's thinking, she doesn't hand it to you as a question.

"Go on, then," she says simply. "I'll be here."

<<link "Step through alone" "Ch2_Trial_Chamber">>
	<<personality "faith" 1>>
	<<set $trial.progress += 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Chamber {"position":"1525,2475","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 2 — SCENE 8
The Empty Chamber
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 8>>

The doorway opens onto a room that is, as far as you can tell, entirely empty.

No pedestal. No light. No Seal waiting on an altar the way you'd half-expected despite yourself. Just bare stone, and your own breathing, and the settling realization that you've bled and climbed and walked through the dark for nothing you can see.

You stand there long enough that it stops feeling like a delay and starts feeling like an answer.

"There's nothing here," you say, when Basim's footsteps finally reach the doorway behind you.

"No," he agrees, unbothered. "There hasn't been, for several hours now."

<<link "Ask what he means" "Ch2_Trial_Seal">><</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Seal {"position":"1525,2525","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 2 — SCENE 8 (cont.)
The Seal of Resolve
==================================================*/

"The trial ended a long way back," Basim says, "somewhere around the second turn back you didn't take, if I had to guess." A faint smile. "I wasn't testing whether you could climb, or see in the dark, or outlast a bad ledge. I was testing whether you'd keep walking once you stopped believing there was a reward waiting for you at the end of it."

<<if $trial.attempts gt 0>>
	"You needed more than one pass at it," he adds, not unkindly. "That doesn't diminish it. Most people do."
<</if>>

"That's Resolve," he says. "Not strength. Not cleverness. Just the choosing, over and over, long after the choosing stopped being easy."

He reaches into his robe and withdraws a smooth white stone disc, palm-sized, a single unbroken circle worn into its face and old script circling its edge. It isn't glowing. It isn't doing anything at all, except sitting warm in his open palm, as if it had been carried against a body for a very long time.

"I've had this the entire walk," he says. "It was never hidden from you. Only from your certainty that it was there."

He holds it out.

<<link "Take the Seal of Resolve" "Ch2_Trial_Aftermath">>
	<<set $seals.first = true>>
	<<set $seals.total += 1>>
	<<run $inventory.keyItems.push("Seal of Resolve")>>
	<<codex "items" "Seal of Resolve">>
	<<relationship "basim" 3>>
	<<personality "faith" 1>>
	<<journal "Sheikh Basim entrusted me with the Seal of Resolve. It was never hidden -- only waiting to see if I'd keep walking without one.">>
	It doesn't feel taken. It feels given -- freely, the way you'd hand something to a person you trusted to carry it, not the way you'd surrender a prize to someone who'd earned the right to take it by force. You close your hand around it. It's warmer than the air.
<</link>>


:: Ch2_Trial_Aftermath {"position":"1525,2575","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 2 — SCENE 9
Aftermath
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 9>>
<<set $trial.active = false>>

Yasmina is waiting exactly where she said she'd be, and whatever question she'd been holding back at the threshold, she doesn't ask it now either. She just looks at the disc in your hand, then at you, and something in her expression has quietly rearranged itself.

"You're different," she says, "than you were going in."

"Good different or bad different?"

"Undecided." But she's smiling when she says it.

As you gather your things to leave, Basim draws her aside for a moment -- not far, not secretive exactly, just a few words pitched too low for you to catch all of. You hear only the tail end of it, not meant for you at all.

"...your road will ask different things," he tells her.

She doesn't explain it to you. You don't ask.

<<link "Climb back to the surface" "Ch2_ChapterEnd">><</link>>


:: Ch2_ChapterEnd {"position":"1525,2625","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 2 — SCENE 9 (end)
Six More Who Remember
==================================================*/

<<set $game.time = "Dusk">>

Above ground, the desert has gone the color of embers, and the staircase behind you is already beginning to soften back into the dune, patient about it, the way it's clearly done a hundred times before.

Basim walks you as far as the old stone marker and no further.

"There are six more who remember," he says, staff planted in the sand like a full stop. "Not six Seals waiting to be collected. Six who will know, the moment you arrive, exactly why you've come -- and decide, each in their own way, whether that's reason enough."

"How do we find them?" Yasmina asks.

"You don't, generally. They find you. Or they let themselves be found, when the finding matters." A pause, and something almost fond crosses his weathered face. "Your scholar father may have better luck than either of us at guessing which."

He rests a hand briefly on your shoulder -- the first time he's touched you since you arrived -- and for just a moment, before he draws it back, something in his weight shifts into you like he needed the steadying more than you did. It passes. Neither of you mentions it.

"Whatever comes next," he says, "remember this, if nothing else. The heart always reaches its destination before the feet. Strength fails a man eventually. So does cleverness, if he leans on it too hard. Resolve is the only one of the three that doesn't."

<<codex "lore" "Basim's Saying: 'The heart always reaches its destination before the feet.'">>
<<objective "Return to Zafirah and consult Hakim Nadir">>
<<journal "Left the First Shrine with the Seal of Resolve. Basim's parting words: 'The heart always reaches its destination before the feet.'">>

<<link "Begin the journey home" "Ch2_ReturnHome">><</link>>


:: Ch2_ReturnHome {"position":"1525,2675","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 2 — EPILOGUE
The Road Home
==================================================*/

<<set $game.day = 8>>
<<set $game.location = "House of Wisdom">>

The road back is easier than the road out, the way return journeys always are once the destination has stopped being a question. Sabiha's caravan carries you as far as the northern gate; the rest of Zafirah does the rest, loud and unbothered and entirely unaware of what's changed.

Hakim doesn't ask how it went. He takes one look at the disc in your palm and sits down rather suddenly, as if his legs had been waiting for permission to stop holding him up.

"Seven divisions," he says, half to himself, already turning back to a wall of hurriedly cross-referenced maps and trade ledgers he's clearly been building since you left. "One found. Six remain, and Basim was right to warn you -- none of them will be found the same way twice."

<<set $knowledge.knowsSevenSeals = true>>
<<set $quest.main = "Recover the Seven Seals">>
<<objective "Await word of the Second Guardian">>

Yasmina sets her satchel down by the door instead of shouldering it. Not tonight, the gesture says. But soon.

"Get some sleep, both of you," Hakim says, not looking up from the maps. "I have a feeling I'm going to need you rested."

<<link "Sleep, and wake to a city that knows your name" "Ch3_Opening">><</link>>

<!-- End of Chapter 2 -->


:: Ch3_Opening {"position":"1625,2775","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 3 — SCENE 1
Echoes in the Dust — the story outgrows you
==================================================*/

<<set $game.chapter = 3>>
<<set $game.scene = 1>>
<<set $game.day = 9>>
<<set $game.time = "Morning">>
<<set $game.location = "Amir's Home">>

By the third morning, the story has outgrown you.

You hear it before you're fully down the stairs -- a neighbor woman in your mother's kitchen, retelling the festival attack with tremendous authority to someone who was actually there. In her version there were three ghūls. In her version you carried the Sultan's own nephew out of the fire. There was no fire. The Sultan has no nephew.

"And now he's off east with a sorceress," the woman finishes, with relish, "carrying a relic worth a province."

"He's off to the market with a basket," Farah says flatly, "carrying my shopping. Amir, bread and onions. Go."

Outside, the Lantern Quarter has learned your name in a way it never needed to before. It used to know you as Farah's boy. Now children point. A man you've never met claps your shoulder like an old debt repaid. Somewhere behind you, someone repeats the thing about the nephew.

"The fishmonger bowed to me yesterday," Farah told you last night, with the expression of a woman describing a crime. "I've known him since he had one tooth. I told him to stop."

Stories, you're beginning to understand, travel faster than the people they're about -- and they don't ask permission before they change.

<<goto "Ch3_Rumors_Hub">>


:: Ch3_Rumors_Hub {"position":"1725,2775","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 3 — SCENE 1 (cont.)
The Quarter, listening back
==================================================*/

<<set _visitedAll = ($choices.sawRumorBilal and $choices.sawRumorPilgrim and $choices.sawRumorCaptain)>>

<<if !$choices.ch3RumorsHubSeen>>
	<<set $choices.ch3RumorsHubSeen = true>>
	The market can wait a little. The street, apparently, cannot -- it has questions, and opinions, and versions of your own week to tell you about.
<<else>>
	The street isn't finished with you yet.
<</if>>

<<if !$choices.sawRumorBilal>>
* <<link "Old Bilal is holding court outside his shop" "Ch3_Rumors_Bilal">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawRumorPilgrim>>
* <<link "A dusty traveler has been asking the neighbors where you live" "Ch3_Rumors_Pilgrim">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawRumorCaptain>>
* <<link "The guard captain from the market is watching you, arms crossed" "Ch3_Rumors_Captain">><</link>>
<</if>>

<<link "Head for the House of Wisdom -- Hakim sent word at dawn" "Ch3_Study">><</link>>


:: Ch3_Rumors_Bilal {"position":"1825,2725","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Rumors: Bilal's version
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawRumorBilal = true>>

Bilal has an audience of six and is not letting facts slow him down. In his telling, you stood on the dais itself, between the ghūl and the Sultan, and the lanterns overhead burned brighter as you fought -- "brighter, I tell you, I make lanterns, I *notice*."

"I wasn't anywhere near the dais," you say.

"You were in spirit." He waves you off without a flicker of shame. "I sell lanterns, boy. You think I don't know how light spreads?"

<<link "Leave him to it" "Ch3_Rumors_Hub">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
	You leave him to it. The story will do what stories do, whether you stand here objecting or not -- and his version, at least, is told with love.
<</link>>

<<link "Correct the record, loudly, for the audience" "Ch3_Rumors_Bilal_Correct">>
<</link>>


:: Ch3_Rumors_Bilal_Correct {"position":"1925,2725","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Rumors: correcting Bilal
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<personality "honor" 1>>

"There was one ghūl," you tell the audience, "I was nowhere near the dais, and the bravest thing I saw that night was a mother running *toward* the screaming."

A respectful pause.

"That's the modesty," Bilal tells his audience, nodding gravely. "They all have it. The great ones." The audience nods back. You give up.

<<link "Give up" "Ch3_Rumors_Hub">><</link>>


:: Ch3_Rumors_Pilgrim {"position":"1825,2775","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Rumors: the pilgrim woman
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawRumorPilgrim = true>>

She's older than Farah, road-worn, and she's been waiting near your door since before you came out -- you can tell by how the neighbors have already given her a stool and tea, because this is still that kind of street.

"You're the one," she says. Not a question. "They say you carry something holy. My son is sick in Wadi Nur." She holds out one cracked hand, palm up, asking without asking. "Let me touch it. For his sake, not mine."

The Seal sits in your breast pocket, ordinary as a river stone. Whatever the stories say it is, you know what it isn't.

<<link "Let her hold it a moment" "Ch3_Rumors_Hub">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
	<<journal "A pilgrim asked to touch the Seal for her sick son. I let her. I don't know what it did for him. I know what it did for her.">>
	You put the white stone in her hands. It does nothing at all -- no light, no warmth beyond what your own body left in it. She closes her eyes anyway, and whatever she takes from that moment, she takes it with both hands and gives you back the stone like it weighs more now.

	You don't have the heart to tell her it's just a stone. You're no longer entirely sure it would be true.
<</link>>

<<link "Tell her gently that it's not what the stories say" "Ch3_Rumors_Hub">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	"It's a stone, auntie," you say, as gently as the words allow. "An old promise, maybe. Not a cure."

	"Promises have mended more sons than physicians have," she says, unbothered, and pats your hand as if *you* were the one needing comfort, and goes.
<</link>>


:: Ch3_Rumors_Captain {"position":"1825,2825","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Rumors: the guard captain, unconvinced
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawRumorCaptain = true>>

<<if $choices.sawGuardCaptain>>
	The guard captain from the marketplace hasn't changed -- same crossed arms, same eyes that never stop doing arithmetic on a crowd. She nods at you the way she did before any of this happened, which is worth more than the bowing.
<<else>>
	A guard captain you half-recognize from the marketplace is watching you with her arms crossed and the expression of a woman auditing a ledger that doesn't add up.
<</if>>

"Half the city says you saved it," she says. "The other half says whatever you dug up out there is what drew the thing in the first place. Both halves buy more bread either way, so the bakers are happy."

"And you?"

"I don't deal in halves." She looks you over once, professionally. "Chase your stories east if you must. Just don't drag the Black Vizier's luck back through my gate."

It's an old curse -- something grandmothers say about spilled salt and late accounts. No one so much as blinks at it, you least of all.

<<link "Promise her you'll try" "Ch3_Rumors_Hub">>
	<<personality "honor" 1>>
	"I'll try," you say, and mean it, and she accepts that the way she'd accept a watch report -- noted, filed, unsentimental.
<</link>>

<<link "Ask what she thinks drew the ghūl" "Ch3_Rumors_Hub">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
	She's quiet a moment, which from her is a speech. "Nineteen years on these walls. Nothing ever came up through the stones before." She looks past you, toward the square. "Whatever changed, it didn't change here. It changed somewhere else first, and we got the weather from it."

	You'll think about that sentence for days.
<</link>>


:: Ch3_Study {"position":"1725,2875","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 3 — SCENE 2
The pattern in the ink
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 2>>
<<set $game.location = "House of Wisdom">>

Hakim's study has stopped pretending to be a room and committed fully to being an argument between paper and gravity. Yasmina is in the middle of it, cross-legged on the one clear patch of floor, surrounded by open travelers' accounts in five different hands, and she doesn't say good morning. She says:

"It was never the ghūl's mark."

She turns a ledger toward you. A merchant's route-diary, sixty years old, describing a caravansary with a symbol scratched above the lintel -- *a circle, broken in seven places*. Then another, older: a well gone dry, the same mark on its coping stone, copied by a pilgrim who thought it was a ward against thieves. Then a third, older still, in a hand so faded it's more suggestion than script.

"Different decades. Different roads. Different writers who never met." Her finger moves from page to page. "The ink ages. The hand changes. The question doesn't. Someone has been visiting the old places and leaving the same mark -- or looking for it -- for *generations*, Amir. The ghūl wasn't the beginning of anything. It was just the first time the search got loud enough for anyone to hear."

Hakim, from the depths of his shelves, without turning: "Tell him where the accounts converge."

"East." Her eyes have that lit-from-inside look. "Three of these mention a library. Older than Zafirah -- older than the *Sultanate*. Half-buried, roof fallen in, abandoned so long ago that no one alive remembers it working. The road-books call it the Sunken Library." A beat. "Whoever's been searching, they searched there. If the mark means anything, that's where somebody wrote it down."

<<objective "Travel east to the Sunken Library">>
<<journal "Yasmina found the broken-circle mark in travelers' accounts spanning generations. Someone has been searching the old places for a very long time. The accounts converge on a ruined library east of the city.">>

She's already packing, you notice. Books, to go and look at books.

<<link "Leave at first light" "Ch3_Road">><</link>>


:: Ch3_Road {"position":"1725,2975","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 3 — SCENE 3
The road east, and a fire
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 3>>
<<set $game.day = 10>>
<<set $game.time = "Night">>
<<set $game.location = "The Eastern Road">>

The eastern road is a day of flat scrubland and one range of low hills that take longer than they look, and by dusk you've made camp in the lee of a boundary stone so old its inscription has gone the way of all inscriptions.

Yasmina reads until the light gives out, then closes the book and doesn't reach for a lamp -- which, you've learned, is her version of announcing she's willing to talk.

The fire cracks. Overhead, the stars are doing what stars do out here, which is everything.

<<link "Ask her what she's hoping is out there" "Ch3_Road_Continue">>
	<<set $choices.ch3CampTalk = "hope">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
	<<relationship "yasmina" 2>>
	She's quiet long enough that you think she might not answer.

	"Proof that somebody wrote things down because they mattered," she says finally. "Not for a patron. Not for a court. Because they looked at something true and couldn't stand for it to be forgotten." She pulls her blanket tighter. "Every book I've ever loved was written by someone like that. I'd like to stand where they stood. Once."
<</link>>

<<link "Ask her who she thinks is ahead of them" "Ch3_Road_Continue">>
	<<set $choices.ch3CampTalk = "rival">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	<<relationship "yasmina" 1>>
	You ask it plainly, the way you'd ask her to read a difficult line -- because you want *her* reading of it, not your own guess repeated back.

	She counts it off on ink-stained fingers. "They read old scripts, or travel with someone who does. They're funded -- generations of searching costs money. And they've never once announced themselves, which means either they're afraid of something..." she looks at the fire, "...or they're patient. I don't know which is worse."

	Then, with a sidelong look: "You're asking me first now. Before you decide things." A pause. "Don't stop."
<</link>>

<<link "Say nothing, and let the quiet be enough" "Ch3_Road_Continue">>
	<<set $choices.ch3CampTalk = "quiet">>
	<<personality "faith" 1>>
	<<relationship "yasmina" 1>>
	You don't fill the silence, and neither does she, and somewhere in the not-filling, the silence stops being an absence and becomes a thing you're doing together.

	"This is nice," she says eventually, to the fire, in the tone of a scholar conceding a point she hadn't planned to concede.
<</link>>


:: Ch3_Road_Continue {"position":"1825,2975","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Scene 3 (cont.) — morning
--------------------------------------------------*/

Morning comes early and cold. By midday the scrubland has given way to a country of pale, wind-carved stone, and the road -- what's left of it -- runs between outcrops like a memory of itself.

Then the land drops away in front of you, and there it is.

<<link "The Sunken Library" "Ch3_Library_Arrival">><</link>>


:: Ch3_Library_Arrival {"position":"1725,3075","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 3 — SCENE 4
The Sunken Library
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 4>>
<<set $game.day = 11>>
<<set $game.time = "Afternoon">>
<<set $game.location = "The Sunken Library">>
<<set $places.sunkenLibrary.visited = true>>
<<codex "places" "The Sunken Library">>

It fills a hollow between the stone hills the way sleep fills a body.

Columns stand in ranks like a forest that decided, at some point centuries ago, to stop pretending to be anything but beautiful. Half the roof is gone; the rooms beneath the gaps are rooms of open sky, and the sand has moved in through the doorways in long, frozen tides, burying whole wings up to their windows. Swallows have the high shelves now. The light comes down in shafts, and the dust in the shafts moves like slow water.

"Oh," Yasmina says. Just that. Her voice does something on the single syllable that you tactfully do not mention.

It should feel dead. It doesn't. It feels *asleep* -- and knowledge, you think, looking at the ranked empty shelves, doesn't die when a place like this falls. It just gets forgotten, which is a different thing, because forgotten things can be found.

Then you see the footprints.

A neat line of them, crossing the sand-tide below the entrance -- recent enough that the wind hasn't finished with them. Near the threshold, a swept patch where someone knelt to copy the inscription on the doorpost. And on the wall inside, in charcoal, in a fast scholarly hand, someone has written a single line beside the old carved circle:

*WHICH OF THE SEVEN?*

"We're not the first ones here," Yasmina says quietly. "And whoever they are, they're asking our question."

<<journal "Reached the Sunken Library -- beautiful, half-buried, asleep. Someone was here recently: footprints, copied inscriptions, and a question written in charcoal beside the broken circle: WHICH OF THE SEVEN?">>

<<goto "Ch3_Library_Hub">>


:: Ch3_Library_Hub {"position":"1825,3075","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 3 — SCENE 4 (cont.)
Exploring the Library
==================================================*/

<<set _visitedAll = ($choices.sawReadingHall and $choices.sawMapRoom and $choices.sawScriptorium)>>

<<if !$choices.ch3LibraryHubSeen>>
	<<set $choices.ch3LibraryHubSeen = true>>
	The library is too large to search all at once, and the light won't last forever. You pick your way in over the frozen tide of sand.
<<else>>
	The stacks wait, patient as only very old things are.
<</if>>

<<if !$choices.sawReadingHall>>
* <<link "The great reading hall, open to the sky" "Ch3_Library_ReadingHall">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawMapRoom>>
* <<link "A map room, half-buried, its cabinets split by time" "Ch3_Library_MapRoom">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if !$choices.sawScriptorium>>
* <<link "The scriptorium, rows of copy-desks under drifted sand" "Ch3_Library_Scriptorium">><</link>>
<</if>>

<<if _visitedAll>>
	Deeper in, past the buried wings, a corridor descends toward the archive stacks -- and from somewhere down its length, faint but unmistakable, comes lamplight. Moving.
<<else>>
	Deeper in, a corridor descends toward the archive stacks -- and from somewhere down its length, faint but unmistakable, comes lamplight. Moving.
<</if>>

<<link "Follow the light down into the stacks" "Ch3_Scholars">><</link>>


:: Ch3_Library_ReadingHall {"position":"1925,3025","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Library: the reading hall
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawReadingHall = true>>
<<codex "lore" "A Child's Copybook">>

The reading hall is the size of a mosque courtyard, and the sky has taken the roof so completely that grass grows between the flagstones where rain must pool in whatever passes for winter here.

Under a collapsed lectern, protected by the very stone that crushed it, Yasmina finds a child's copybook -- a beginner's hand practicing the same line over and over down the page, steadier with each repetition, the way children learn everything:

*What you loved in the light, do not leave in the dark.*
*What you loved in the light, do not leave in the dark.*
*What you loved in the light, do not leave in the dark.*

"A copying exercise," Yasmina says. "A proverb, probably. They always made children practice on proverbs." She sets it down with unusual care, and neither of you says anything else about it.

<<link "Return to the stacks" "Ch3_Library_Hub">>
	<<personality "faith" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch3_Library_MapRoom {"position":"1925,3075","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Library: the map room
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawMapRoom = true>>
<<codex "lore" "The Old Survey Fragment">>

The map cabinets split their seams a century ago, and most of what they held went to dust and beetles long before you were born. But vellum outlives paper, and one surveyor's fragment has survived in the bottom drawer like a coin at the bottom of a dry well.

It shows the stone country you crossed yesterday -- recognizable even now -- and beyond it, eastward, mountains drawn in the old style, all teeth. Against one peak, a tiny square tower, and beside it three words in a script even Yasmina has to work at.

"*The watching...*" She angles it to the light. "The second word is wind. The first I'd have to check against Father's grammars." She looks up. "Someone surveyed a fortress out there. Long enough ago that the mountains had different names."

She takes a careful rubbing, the way her father taught her, and stows it flat between board covers.

<<link "Return to the stacks" "Ch3_Library_Hub">>
	<<personality "curiosity" 1>>
<</link>>


:: Ch3_Library_Scriptorium {"position":"1925,3125","size":"100,100"}
/*--------------------------------------------------
Library: the scriptorium — Yasmina, happy
--------------------------------------------------*/

<<set $choices.sawScriptorium = true>>
<<relationship "yasmina" 1>>

The scriptorium is rows of stone copy-desks under a foot of drifted sand, and Yasmina walks into it the way other people walk into gardens.

"Forty copyists," she says, moving down the rows, brushing sand from a desk edge, "maybe fifty. Ink here -- see the stains, they never come out, my hands look the same." She laughs at a discovery you don't understand yet: "The floor. Look at the floor by this desk -- worn in a crescent, where students stood *waiting*. This was the master's desk. Apprentices lined up here to have their pages checked, shifting their feet, for -- what, a hundred years? Two?"

She looks up at you, sand on her knees, dust in her hair, ink ghosts on her fingers from three days of handling old paper, and she is, you realize, entirely and unselfconsciously happy -- happier than you've ever seen her, here in a dead room full of absent people, hearing everything they left behind.

"Don't say anything," she says, reading your face. "I know what I look like."

"You look like you're home."

"...That," she allows, "was better than what I expected you to say."

<<link "Return to the stacks" "Ch3_Library_Hub">><</link>>


:: Ch3_Scholars {"position":"1725,3175","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 3 — SCENE 5
The gray scholars
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 5>>
<<set $game.time = "Dusk">>

You come down into the archive stacks quietly, and the argument reaches you before the light does.

"--the shelf-marks run right to left, I've *told* you--"

Five of them, gray-robed, working by lamplight in the deep stacks: two packing crates with careful, gloved hands, two copying wall-inscriptions at speed, and one -- older, spectacled, with the permanent ink calluses of a life spent exactly the way Yasmina intends to spend hers -- directing it all like a man who has waited years for this room.

He sees you. He doesn't reach for a weapon. He takes off his spectacles, which is somehow worse.

"The young man from Zafirah." He polishes the lenses on his sleeve, unhurried. "You've come for the same thing we have, so let us not waste the evening pretending otherwise. The difference between us is honesty: we admit the old knowledge was *taken* from the world. Locked away by self-appointed keepers who decided the rest of us couldn't be trusted with it." He resettles the spectacles. "Your Guardians nailed a door shut and called the nails holy. We've come to pull the nails. Knowledge belongs to everyone."

Behind him, the crates fill, page by careful page.

<<link "Answer him yourself" "Ch3_Scholars_Fight">>
	<<set $choices.ch3Debate = "amir">>
	<<personality "honor" 1>>
	"I've met one of your self-appointed keepers," you say. "He'd have answered any question you asked him. You never went and asked. You came here instead, at night, with crates." You nod at the packing. "That's not freeing knowledge. That's robbing a grave and calling it a rescue."

	Something flickers behind the spectacles -- not shame. Calculation.
<</link>>

<<link "Look to Yasmina -- this is her ground, not yours" "Ch3_Scholars_Fight">>
	<<set $choices.ch3Debate = "yasmina">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	<<relationship "yasmina" 2>>
	You hold your tongue and glance at Yasmina -- and she steps past you like a blade coming out of a sheath, if blades were made of citations.

	"You're copying right-to-left shelf-marks left-to-right," she says. "You've crated water-damaged folios with sound ones, which will rot the lot by spring. And you're *burning lamp-oil next to eight-hundred-year-old paper.*" Her voice doesn't rise; it sharpens. "You don't want knowledge to belong to everyone. You want it to belong to *you*, and you can't even read it. That's not scholarship. It's looting with a vocabulary."

	The old man's jaw tightens, one notch.
<</link>>

<<link "Skip the debate -- tell them to put it all back and go" "Ch3_Scholars_Fight">>
	<<set $choices.ch3Debate = "warning">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
	"I'm not going to argue philosophy in a burning-oil light next to dry paper," you say. "Put it back. All of it. Walk out the way you came, and nobody has to find out tonight what any of us is willing to do."

	For one moment -- you'd swear it -- the old man almost considers it.
<</link>>


:: Ch3_Scholars_Fight {"position":"1825,3175","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
SCENE 5 (cont.) — the scuffle in the stacks: Round 1
==================================================*/

<<set $combat.active = true>>
<<set $combat.round = 1>>

Then the moment passes. The old man makes a small, tired gesture -- *take them* -- and the two copyists drop their charcoals and come at you with the awkward, committed violence of men who trained for this late in life and hoped never to need it.

It's nothing like the ghūl. That was a nightmare in a crowd. This is close, and human, and clumsy, and somehow worse for it -- knocking through shelves that have stood eight hundred years, in a room built by people who believed writing things down mattered.

<<link "Put yourself between them and Yasmina" "Ch3_Scholars_Fight2">>
	<<set $choices.ch3FightFirst = "yasmina">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
	<<damage 5>>
	You take a copyist's wild swing on your forearm rather than let it travel past you to where she stands, and answer it with the flat of your sword and a shove that sits him down hard among the sand-drifts. Yasmina, to her credit, uses the shelter of you to snatch a crated folio back out of a gray sleeve.
<</link>>

<<link "Defend the crates -- the pages are what everyone's here for" "Ch3_Scholars_Fight2">>
	<<set $choices.ch3FightFirst = "crates">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	You plant yourself between the crates and everyone else, and the fight comes to you on your terms. Whatever else happens in this room tonight, the pages will not leave it -- and the gray men's whole plan bends awkwardly around that single fixed fact.
<</link>>

<<link "Go straight for the old man" "Ch3_Scholars_Fight2">>
	<<set $choices.ch3FightFirst = "leader">>
	<<personality "honor" 1>>
	<<damage 10>>
	Cut off the head. You shoulder through the two copyists -- taking an elbow across the ear for it that you'll feel for days -- and put your blade's point in the lamplight a hand's width from the old man's chest, close enough that the whole room goes still around the two of you.
<</link>>


:: Ch3_Scholars_Fight2 {"position":"1925,3175","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
SCENE 5 (end) — Round 2: how it ends
==================================================*/

<<set $combat.round = 2>>

The fight was decided before it started -- they're scholars with knives, and you're the man from the festival square, whatever the stories exaggerated -- and everyone in the room arrives at that understanding at roughly the same moment.

What's left is how it ends.

<<link "Disarm, don't wound -- these are men, not ghūls" "Ch3_Scholars_End">>
	<<set $choices.ch3FightEnd = "disarm">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
	You beat the knife from the nearest copyist's grip so hard his whole arm goes numb, and catch the second man's wrist and simply hold it, immovable, until the fight goes out of him like water out of a cracked jar. No one bleeds. It costs you more effort than hurting them would have. It's worth it.
<</link>>

<<link "Drive them toward the broken doors and out" "Ch3_Scholars_End">>
	<<set $choices.ch3FightEnd = "rout">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	You give them a direction to flee and make every other direction worse, herding gray robes through the stacks the way the city guard herds festival drunks -- with momentum, noise, and the strong suggestion of consequences.
<</link>>

<<link "Put the old man down and demand their surrender" "Ch3_Scholars_End">>
	<<set $choices.ch3FightEnd = "surrender">>
	<<personality "honor" 1>>
	You put the old man on his knees with an efficiency that surprises even you, and pitch your voice the way the guard captain pitches hers: "*Enough.* Knives down. All of you." And -- because it is that kind of voice, apparently, now -- knives go down.
<</link>>


:: Ch3_Scholars_End {"position":"2025,3175","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
SCENE 5 (aftermath) — the one who got away
==================================================*/

<<set $combat.active = false>>
<<set $combat.victory = true>>

It ends the way these things end: two of them kneeling in the sand-drift with their hands visible, one groaning under a toppled (empty, thank God) shelf, the old man's spectacles hanging from one ear.

And one -- the youngest, thin as a reed, the one you never saw draw a knife at all -- already twenty feet up the great sand-slope that pours through the fallen roof, climbing like a lizard with a satchel slung across his back.

"*Stop him--*" the old man croaks -- not to you. To the boy. "*Not to them! Not like this--*"

The boy doesn't stop. At the lip of the broken roof he's a silhouette against the last of the dusk, and then he's gone, and the only thing left of him is a handful of loose pages spiraling down through the violet air.

One lands at your feet. A copied inscription, half-finished: a circle, broken in seven places. And in the margin, in a quick, frightened hand, a single question:

*which is first?*

<<journal "We fought the gray scholars in the Sunken Library's stacks. Most surrendered or fled -- but the youngest escaped through the broken roof carrying a satchel of copied pages. The old man shouted after him: 'Not to them.' Whoever 'they' are.">>

<<link "See to the ones who remain" "Ch3_Journal">><</link>>


:: Ch3_Journal {"position":"1725,3275","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 3 — SCENE 6
The damaged journal
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 6>>
<<set $game.time = "Night">>

The room the gray scholars were ransacking turns out to be a records-room -- ledgers, registries, the administrative bones of the library's daily life, which is exactly the kind of thing looters ignore and Yasmina goes through like a woman possessed.

Which is how she finds it: wedged behind a split shelf-board where it slid, or was hidden, a lifetime of lifetimes ago. A journal. Leather-bound, water-stained, the size of two hands.

She reads by lamplight -- carefully, far from the oil -- and her voice changes three sentences in.

"Amir. This was written by someone who *knew* one of them." She doesn't look up. "Listen. '*Took the north track to the fortress with the autumn provisioning -- oil, salt, thread, his winter wool. He met me at the wall as always. Ninth year I have made this climb, and he looks no older, and I have stopped asking.*'" Her finger moves down the ruined page. "'*He laughed at my complaining like anyone laughs. That is the thing I can never make the others believe. They want him to be a story. He is only the most faithful man I have ever met.*'"

She turns the page. Then turns it back. Then checks the binding, and her jaw goes tight.

"The last pages are gone. Not rotted -- *cut*. Rot is patient; this was scissors." She holds the journal open to the stub-edges, clean and deliberate. "Someone read this before us. Maybe generations before us. And they took the ending."

But the cut missed one line, on the last surviving leaf -- the writer's plain, unremarkable habit of noting a destination:

*...set out at dawn for the fortress that watches the wind.*

<<run $inventory.keyItems.push("Damaged Journal")>>
<<codex "items" "The Damaged Journal">>
<<codex "lore" "The Fortress That Watches the Wind">>
<<journal "Yasmina found a journal written by someone who personally knew a Guardian -- provisioned him for years, watched him not age, loved him like family. The final pages were cut out. One line survives: 'the fortress that watches the wind.'">>

"They were real," Yasmina says quietly, closing the journal like something living. "Not myths. Not allegories. Someone packed a man's winter wool and climbed a mountain to him for nine years." She looks up at you. "And someone else cut out the ending and has been hunting the rest of it ever since."

<<link "Deal with the prisoners" "Ch3_Aftermath">><</link>>


:: Ch3_Aftermath {"position":"1825,3275","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 3 — SCENE 7
What to do with beaten men
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 7>>

The old man sits against a pillar with his broken spectacles in his lap, and up close, in the honest light of the lamp, he is nothing like a servant of darkness. He's a tired scholar with sand in his beard and a lifetime of being almost-respected curdling behind his eyes.

"We were *invited*," he says, to no one, before you've asked anything. "Letters. Learned letters -- citations, shelf-marks, questions no living scholar should have known to ask. Coin for expenses. Lists of places." A bitter little laugh. "Never a name. Never a face. I told myself it was a patron who valued discretion. A man believes what dignifies him."

"And the boy?" Yasmina asks. "Where do the pages go?"

"A drop-point on the pilgrim road. Then wherever they go." He finally looks up. "You think you're in a race with us. We thought we were in a race with *you*. I no longer believe either of us knows who is actually running."

<<journal "The gray scholars were recruited by letters -- learned, funded, faceless. They never met their patron. The old man no longer believes either of us knows who is actually in this race.">>

<<link "Let them go, with water enough to reach the road" "Ch3_NightCamp">>
	<<set $choices.ch3ScholarsFate = "released">>
	<<personality "mercy" 1>>
	You give them water, and their own feet, and the advice to answer no more learned letters. The old man pauses at the edge of the lamplight, looks back at the shelves -- at everything he told himself he was saving -- and has the grace, at last, to look ashamed.
<</link>>

<<link "March them to the waystation garrison, a day south" "Ch3_NightCamp">>
	<<set $choices.ch3ScholarsFate = "handed">>
	<<personality "discipline" 1>>
	<<reputation "city" 1>>
	They walk ahead of you to the waystation without complaint -- relieved, you suspect, to have the decision taken from them. The garrison sergeant takes charge of them and writes your name in his ledger with the studied casualness of a man who will be retelling this at dinner for a month.
<</link>>


:: Ch3_NightCamp {"position":"1725,3375","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 3 — SCENE 8
The fire in the hall of sky
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 8>>
<<set $game.time = "Night">>
<<set $game.location = "The Sunken Library">>

You make camp in the great reading hall, under the missing roof, where grass grows between the flagstones and the stars have taken over the shelving.

"Lay it out for me," you say. "All of it. You've been connecting things all day -- I've watched you doing it. I want to hear it the way you see it."

Yasmina looks at you for a moment -- surprised, you think, though she'd deny it -- and then she builds it for you in the firelight, piece by piece, the way her father builds an argument:

"Someone has searched the old places for generations -- the marks in the route-books prove that. Someone cut the ending out of that journal, maybe a hundred years ago, maybe more. Someone taught last month's ghūl to come up through a drain in the one city where a festival would hide its approach." She turns the charcoal page with the copied circle toward the fire. "And someone -- maybe the same someone, maybe not -- is *funding confused scholars by anonymous letter* to strip these places bare and send the pages down a pilgrim road to nowhere."

She looks up.

"Someone doesn't want the past read, Amir. And someone else is reading it anyway. And we are standing exactly between them, holding a stone."

The fire cracks. You think about the captain's line -- *it changed somewhere else first, and we got the weather from it* -- and say nothing, and listen, because you're learning that listening is a thing you can be better at than anyone expects of a young man with a sword.

"We," you say eventually. Not a question. Just checking the weight of it.

"Obviously," she says, already unrolling her blanket. "You'd misread every shelf-mark without me."

<<journal "Yasmina laid the whole pattern out by firelight. Someone doesn't want the past read; someone else is reading it anyway. We're between them. 'We,' it turns out, no longer needs saying.">>

<<link "Sleep, and start home at dawn" "Ch3_Return">><</link>>


:: Ch3_Return {"position":"1725,3475","size":"100,100"}
/*==================================================
CHAPTER 3 — SCENE 9
Three sources
==================================================*/

<<set $game.scene = 9>>
<<set $game.day = 13>>
<<set $game.time = "Night">>
<<set $game.location = "House of Wisdom">>

Hakim reads the journal twice before he says anything at all. When he finally speaks, it's to the bookshelf, the way he does when the thought is too large to aim at a person.

"The fortress that watches the wind." He shakes his head slowly. "I have read every map in this city worth the name, and it is on none of them. No tax roll. No garrison list. Nothing."

"But?" Yasmina says, who knows her father's *but* the way sailors know weather.

"But." He pulls down a battered folio and opens it to a soldier's pension-scroll, generations old, its clerk's hand crabbed and formal: *paid out his service at the fortress that watches the wind, and released.* "One. And now your journal -- two. Independent hands, independent years."

<<if $choices.sawMapRoom>>
	Yasmina is already unrolling her rubbing from the map room -- the surveyor's fragment, the tiny square tower against mountains drawn like teeth, the three words in the old script. She lays it on the desk without a word and permits herself exactly one small, insufferable smile.

	"*Three*," Hakim says, and for a moment he looks twenty years younger.
<<else>>
	Hakim digs further, muttering, and surfaces at last with a caravan song written down by some bored customs clerk a lifetime ago -- a walking-verse about roads' ends: *...and the wind goes home to the fortress that watches it.* "A child's rhyme. But it makes *three*, in three different hands, across three different generations."
<</if>>

"Three sources," Yasmina says. "No modern map. A place real enough that soldiers were pensioned there and provisioners climbed to it every autumn -- and forgotten so thoroughly that the forgetting itself looks deliberate."

"East," Hakim says. "Where the desert breaks against the mountains. Barren country. Hard travel." He looks at the two of you over his spectacles, and whatever he sees apparently settles something, because the worry in his face loses -- not the fear, but the argument.

"Whoever cut those pages has had a very long start," he says. "And whoever bought last week's scholars reads faster than you walk. Leave early."

<<objective "Find the Fortress That Watches the Wind">>
<<journal "Three independent sources name the Fortress That Watches the Wind, east where the desert meets the mountains. No modern map records it. For the first time, we know where we're going before we understand why -- and we are not the only ones who know.">>

Outside, Zafirah is lighting its ordinary evening lamps, one window at a time, a city-full of people who believe the strangest thing in the world is the price of onions. You find you love them for it -- and that the love has weight now, like a stone carried against the chest.

Somewhere east, past the last of the maps, an old fortress is watching the wind come and go.

Time to see what it's been waiting for.

<!-- End of Chapter 3 -->
